/* Generated by tools/build-search-index.js. Do not edit by hand. */
PL.searchIndex = {"v":1,"lessons":{"programs":{"k":["program","code","coding","instructions","algorithm","print","comment","bug","debugging","hello world","getting started","beginner"],"s":[["A program is a recipe","Meet Chef Bot. It can't think for itself — it just follows instructions, one line at a time, from top to bottom. A list of instructions like this is called a program. Press Run to watch it cook."],["Order matters","Uh-oh — someone shuffled the recipe. Run it to see what Chef Bot makes, then use the arrows to put the lines in the right order."],["Computers take you literally","A person would guess what you meant. A computer won't. Each recipe below has one tiny mistake that would confuse Chef Bot. Programmers call mistakes bugs. Tap the buggy line."],["Making the computer talk: print()","print() tells the computer to show something on the screen. Put the text you want inside the brackets — wrapped in quotes. Pick a message (or type your own) and run it."],["Quotes change everything","What's the difference between print(\"7 + 5\") and print(7 + 5)? Flip between them and watch what Chef Bot says."],["Notes for humans: comments","Any line that starts with # is a comment. Python skips it completely — it's a note for people reading the code. Programmers also use # to quickly switch a line off. Tap the # buttons to switch lines off, then run."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions to lock it in. Read each program top to bottom, just like the computer does."]],"r":["A program is a list of instructions. The computer follows them one at a time, top to bottom.","Order matters — the same steps in a different order give a different result.","Computers take you literally. A typo, a capital letter or a missing bracket is a bug.","print() makes the computer show something on the screen.","Text in quotes is shown exactly as written; without quotes, Python calculates it first.","Lines starting with # are comments — notes for humans that Python skips."],"rel":[]},"variables":{"k":["variable","var","assignment","assign","store","remember a value","box","data types","int","str","bool","reassign","counter"],"s":[["Computers need to remember things","A game remembers your score. Your phone remembers your name. When Python needs to remember something, it puts it in a variable — think of it as a box with a label on it."],["Anatomy of a variable","Here is one line of real Python. It makes a box called name and puts the text \"Ada\" inside. Tap each part of the line to see what it does."],["Make your own variables","You get to name your boxes — but Python has a few naming rules. Type a name, pick a value, and store it. Try the broken names to see what Python dislikes."],["Three kinds of values","Boxes can hold different kinds of values. Python treats them differently, so it's important to tell them apart. Tap a value, then tap the bin it belongs in (or drag it)."],["A box holds one thing at a time","What happens if you store a value in a box that already has something in it? Step through this program and watch the box called mood."],["Variables made from variables","You can use what's inside one box to fill another. But be careful — this one trips up lots of beginners. Make a prediction first."],["Updating a value: score = score + 1","This line looks impossible in maths — but in Python it's one of the most common lines you'll ever write. Python always works out the right side first, then stores the answer."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions to lock in what you've learned. No pressure — you can try as many times as you like."]],"r":["A variable is a labelled box that holds one value.","= means store: put the right side into the box on the left.","Values come in kinds: numbers 42, text \"hi\", and booleans True / False.","Storing a new value replaces the old one.","score = score + 1 reads the old value, computes, then stores the result."],"rel":["potions","scope","refs"]},"numbers":{"k":["math","arithmetic","maths","calculator","integer division","floor division","modulo","remainder","exponent","power","precedence","bodmas","pemdas","operators","float","decimal"],"s":[["Python is a calculator","Computers are brilliant at maths. Pick an operator and slide the numbers — the candies show what the maths means, and Python shows the answer. Note: Python uses * for times and / for divide."],["Sharing candy: // and %","You have a bag of candies to share fairly between friends. Everyone must get the same amount — the rest stays in the jar. Python has two operators for exactly this: // and %."],["Powers: the magic beans","A magic bean doubles every day. Day 0: 1 bean. Day 1: 2 beans. Day 2: 4… In Python, \"multiply by itself again and again\" is written with ** (to the power of)."],["Who goes first?","What is 2 + 3 * 4? Is it 20 or 14? Python follows a fixed rule about which operation goes first — like a queue where some operators have a VIP pass. Step through and watch."],["Shortcuts: += and friends","Updating a variable is so common that Python has shortcuts. coins += 5 means exactly the same as coins = coins + 5. Puzzle: you start with 10 coins. Use the buttons to reach exactly 50."],["Maths inside a program","Let's put it together. 23 candies, 4 friends. Predict what this program prints, then watch it run. (When print gets two values separated by a comma, it shows both with a space between.)"],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions. Remember the VIP queue: brackets, then * / // %, then + -."]],"r":["+ - * / add, subtract, multiply and divide. / always gives a decimal (a float), like 4.0.","// divides and drops the remainder: 17 // 5 is 3.","% gives just the remainder: 17 % 5 is 2.","` means \"to the power of\": 2 3 is 2 × 2 × 2 = 8`.","Python does brackets first, then * /, then + -.","coins += 5 is a shortcut for coins = coins + 5."],"rel":[]},"strings":{"k":["string","text","concatenation","concatenate","f-string","format","quotes","length","len"],"s":[["Text lives in quotes","Programs work with text all the time: names, messages, passwords. Python calls a piece of text a string — think of letters strung together like beads. Type your name to make a badge."],["Gluing text together","Use + to glue strings into a longer one. Tap the word magnets to build an expression. Careful — Python glues exactly what you give it."],["Repeating text with *","Here's a fun one: * works on text too. \"ha\" * 3 repeats \"ha\" three times. Meet the laugh-o-meter."],["How long is it? len()","Every character in a string sits in its own little slot — letters, digits, emoji, even spaces. len() counts the slots. Type anything and watch."],["Fill in the blanks: f-strings","Want to put values inside a sentence? Put an f before the quotes and write variable names in {curly brackets}. Python fills in each blank. Change the values below and watch the postcard update."],["Text or number?","3 and \"3\" look almost the same — but to Python they're completely different things. Flip the quotes on each value and see what + does."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about strings. Watch out for quotes and spaces!"]],"r":["Text in Python is called a string. It always lives inside quotes: \"Ada\" or 'Ada'.","Without quotes, Python thinks a word is a variable name.","+ glues strings together exactly — it never adds spaces for you.","* repeats a string: \"ha\" * 3 is \"hahaha\".","len() counts the characters — spaces count too!","f-strings fill in blanks: f\"Hi {name}\" puts the value of name into the text.","\"3\" + \"4\" is \"34\", but 3 + 4 is 7. Mixing text and numbers with + is an error."],"rel":["strseq"]},"input":{"k":["input","user input","type conversion","casting","int()","float()","str()","convert","ask the user"],"s":[["Asking a question: input()","So far our programs only talked. input() lets them listen: it shows a question, then waits for the user to type an answer. Press Run and answer the computer."],["input() always gives text","Here's the catch that trips up everyone. Whatever you type — even digits — input() hands it to the program as text. Watch the machine, then see what happens on line 2."],["The 5 + 5 = 55 surprise","Here's a calculator that adds two numbers. Run it and give it 5 and 5. Then flip the switch to fix it with int(), which turns text into a whole number."],["The converter machines","Python has a machine for each type: int() makes whole numbers, float() makes decimals and str() makes text. Pick a capsule, pick a machine, and press Convert. Some combinations will jam the machine!"],["A real program: dog years","Let's build something real. A dog ages about 7 years for every human year. This program asks your age, converts it, does the maths and tells you your age in dog years. Try it with two different ages."],["Spot the bug","A friend wrote a \"double it\" program. The user types 4. Predict what appears."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions to lock in conversions."]],"r":["input(\"question\") shows the question, then waits until the user types an answer and presses Enter.","Whatever the user types, input() always gives back text (a string) — even if they type digits.","\"5\" + \"5\" is \"55\", so convert first: int(input(...)) turns the answer into a whole number.","int() makes whole numbers, float() makes decimals, str() makes text.","int(2.9) is 2 — it chops off the decimal part, it doesn't round.","Converting text that isn't a number, like int(\"hello\"), causes a ValueError."],"rel":[]},"conditionals":{"k":["if","else","elif","condition","conditional","branch","decision","comparison","boolean","true false","compare"],"s":[["Programs make choices","So far our programs ran every line, top to bottom. Real programs choose. Meet our robot: it checks the weather, then picks a path. Flip the switch and send it off."],["Questions with yes/no answers","Before a program can decide, it needs to ask a question whose answer is True or False. We do that with comparisons. Drag the thermometer and watch every answer update."],["The if statement","An if runs a block of code only when its condition is True. Watch the dot travel through the flowchart — it's exactly the path Python takes through the code."],["Otherwise… else","Often you want to do one thing or another. else catches every case where the condition is False. Slide the age and watch the path — and the ticket — change live."],["Many choices: elif","elif is short for \"else if\". It lets you check several conditions in order. Python goes down the list and runs the first one that's True — then skips the rest."],["Order matters","Here's a buggy grading program. The conditions are the same as before — just in a different order. Think about how Python walks down the chain."],["Combining questions: and / or","Sometimes one question isn't enough. To ride this roller coaster you must be at least 10 years old and at least 120 cm tall. Adjust the rider until the gate opens."],["Indentation is the fence","Python uses indentation (spaces at the start of a line) to know which lines are inside an if. Read carefully — then predict what gets printed."]],"r":["Comparisons like age >= 18 are questions whose answer is True or False.","== asks \"is it equal?\" — a single = stores a value.","if runs its indented block only when the condition is True; else handles the other case.","In an elif chain Python checks top to bottom and runs only the first match.","and needs both sides True; or needs at least one.","Indentation decides which lines belong inside the if."],"rel":[]},"logic":{"k":["boolean logic","and","or","not","logical operators","nested if","truth table"],"s":[["Two locks: and","This treasure chest has two locks. It only opens if you have the key and you know the secret code. In Python that's exactly how and works. Try every combination to fill in the table."],["Two doors: or","Whiskers the cat wants to get inside. The house has two doors — if the front door or the back door is open, she can get in. Try every combination."],["Flip it: not","not is the simplest one: it flips a value. not True is False, and not False is True. Put on the invisibility cloak and see."],["Mixing them: the picnic decision","Real decisions combine all three. We'll go on a picnic if it's sunny or warm, and not raining. Python works it out like a tree, from the leaves up. Flip the weather and watch the tree light up."],["Choose your adventure: nested ifs","An if can live inside another if. Our explorer first checks for a map — and only if there is one, checks for a torch. Flip the switches and send the explorer off. Can you reach all three endings?"],["Flatten it with and","Nested ifs can get deep and hard to read. Often you can squash two of them into one line. Which single if does exactly the same job?"],["Checkpoint","Two questions to lock in your logic. Work from the inside out."]],"r":["a and b is True only when both are True — like a chest with two locks.","a or b is True when at least one is True — like a house with two doors.","not a flips True to False and back.","You can combine them, like (sunny or warm) and not raining. Brackets decide what's worked out first.","An if can sit inside another if — that's a nested if.","Two nested ifs can often be flattened into one with and."],"rel":["combine"]},"loops":{"k":["loop","for loop","iterate","iteration","repeat","range","running total","turtle"],"s":[["Don't repeat yourself","Say you want to cheer 10 times. You could copy the same line 10 times… or use a loop. Drag the slider and compare the two programs."],["A loop visits every item","A list is a row of values inside square brackets: [\"🍎\", \"🍌\", \"🍇\"]. A for loop walks along the list and drops each item, one at a time, into a variable box — then runs the indented code."],["Counting with range()","Often you just want to repeat something a number of times. range() makes a sequence of numbers for the loop to walk through. It has one famous quirk: it starts at 0 and stops before the end number."],["Keeping a running total","Loops get powerful when they build something up. Here we start with an empty jar (total = 0) and drop in each coin. This pattern is everywhere: shopping carts, step counters, game scores."],["Loops + decisions","Put an if inside a loop and you can act on only some items. Here we check a week of temperatures and shout about the hot ones. Predict first."],["Draw with loops","Python has a drawing robot called the turtle. forward() moves it, left() turns it. Repeat \"move, turn\" in a loop and shapes appear — this is where loops get beautiful."],["Final challenge","Put it all together: variables, updating, and loops. Trace the code in your head — or grab a pencil!"]],"r":["A for loop repeats its indented block once for every item.","Each round, the loop variable (like fruit) holds the current item.","range(5) gives 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 — it starts at 0 and stops before 5.","range(2, 6) gives 2, 3, 4, 5.","Start a total at 0 and add to it inside the loop to build a running total.","You can put an if inside a loop to act only on some items."],"rel":["generators","comps"]},"while":{"k":["while loop","infinite loop","countdown","repeat until","guessing game"],"s":[["Keep going while…","A for loop walks through a list. A while loop is different: it keeps repeating as long as a condition is True. Step through this rocket countdown and watch the condition before every round."],["for or while?","Both loops repeat — so which one should you use? A simple rule: use for when you know the list or how many times; use while to keep going until something changes. Tap a job, then tap the right loop."],["The balloon pump","This loop pumps air into a balloon while it has less than 100. But careful — the balloon pops above 100! Pick how much air each pump adds, then run it. Can you fill it to exactly 100?"],["The loop that never ends","Here's a famous bug. This hamster is supposed to run 10 laps. Run the program — and be ready to hit Stop!"],["Game time: guess my number","The computer has picked a secret number from 1 to 100. The loop keeps asking while your guess is wrong, and tells you to go higher or lower. Can you find it?"],["Predict the doubling","This loop keeps doubling n while it's under 20. Predict where it ends up — careful, it can overshoot!"],["Checkpoint","Two questions about while loops. Trace each one carefully — maybe with a pencil!"]],"r":["A while loop repeats its block as long as its condition is True.","The condition is checked before every round — when it's False, the loop stops.","Use for when you know how many times; use while to keep going until something changes.","If nothing inside the loop ever makes the condition False, you get an infinite loop.","Something inside the loop must move you towards the finish line — like count = count - 1."],"rel":[]},"loopctl":{"k":["break","continue","nested loop","grid","2d","pixel art","exit a loop"],"s":[["Stop early: break","You're hunting for the golden egg. Once you find it, there's no point checking the rest! break jumps straight out of a loop. Tap an egg to hide the golden egg there, then run the hunt."],["Skip one: continue","Now you're packing apples. Rotten ones should be skipped, but the packing must carry on. That's continue: it skips the rest of this round and jumps to the next item. Tap apples to make them rotten, then run."],["break or continue?","Same loop, one word different. Switch between break and continue and watch which numbers get printed."],["Loops inside loops","A loop can contain another loop. Think of a clock: the minute hand goes all the way round for every single step of the hour hand. Here the inner loop paints a whole row for each step of the outer loop."],["Pixel art with nested loops","Put an if inside nested loops and you can draw patterns. Every pixel asks the same question; the answer decides its colour. Pick a rule and paint."],["Count the hellos","Nested loops multiply. Predict how many times the inner line runs."],["Final challenge","Two last questions for this chapter. Trace each loop round by round."]],"r":["break jumps out of the loop immediately — the remaining items are never visited.","continue skips the rest of this round and moves on to the next item.","A loop inside a loop is a nested loop: the inner loop runs completely for every round of the outer one.","Nested loops are perfect for grids: rows × columns.","With an if inside nested loops you can paint patterns, like a chessboard."],"rel":[]},"lists":{"k":["list","array","index","indexing","zero indexed","append","insert","pop","remove","in","IndexError"],"s":[["All aboard!","A list keeps many values together, in order, under one name — like a train pulling cars. Tap the cargo to load it. Each tap runs train.append(...), which hooks a new car onto the end."],["Counting starts at 0","Every car has a position number called its index — and programmers start counting at 0. So the first car is train[0]. Pick an index and see which car Python fetches."],["Swap the cargo","You can replace what's in one car by assigning to its index: train[1] = \"🍉\". The car stays in its place — only the cargo changes. Tap a car, then tap new cargo to match the order sheet."],["Hook on, unhook","Lists have built-in tools called methods, written after a dot: train.append(x). Try all four on the train and watch where each one acts."],["How long? Is it on board?","len(train) counts the cars. x in train asks \"is this cargo anywhere on the train?\" and answers True or False. Ask the train some questions."],["Predict the train","Four list moves in a row. Run them in your head — which two things get printed? Then step through to check."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about lists. Remember: counting starts at 0!"]],"r":["A list holds many values in order: train = [\"🐄\", \"🐑\", \"🐔\"].","Positions are called indexes and start at 0. train[-1] is the last item.","An index past the end gives an IndexError.","train[1] = \"🍉\" replaces one item; the list keeps its length.","append adds to the end, insert adds anywhere, pop removes the last, remove removes by value.","len(train) counts the items; x in train checks whether x is there."],"rel":["stacks","refs","comps"]},"listwork":{"k":["slice","slicing","sort","sorted","sum","min","max","list methods","playlist"],"s":[["Slice a playlist","A slice cuts out part of a list: songs[start:stop]. Think of the numbers as cut marks between the songs — you get everything between the two cuts. Move the cuts to make each mix."],["Slice shortcuts","Leave out start and the slice begins at the front. Leave out stop and it runs to the end. Negative numbers count from the back, just like indexes. Tap each shortcut."],["Sorting the playlist","Python can put a list in order for you, in two different ways. sorted(songs) hands you a new sorted list and leaves the original alone. songs.sort() rearranges the original list itself."],["Time the party mix","When a list holds numbers, Python can crunch it in one word: sum(), min(), max() and len(). These are your songs' lengths in minutes. Tap songs to put them in tonight's party mix."],["Build a new list","A super-common pattern: start with an empty list, loop over another list, and append only the items you want. Let's collect the short songs — 3 minutes or less."],["The sort() trap","This one catches almost everybody. Predict what gets printed."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about slicing and sorting. Remember: the stop number is not included."]],"r":["songs[1:4] is a slice: from index 1 up to, but not including, index 4.","songs[:3] = the first three, songs[-2:] = the last two, songs[::2] = every second item.","sorted(songs) returns a new sorted list; songs.sort() sorts the original and returns None.","sum(), min(), max() and len() summarise a list of numbers in one word.","Build a new list with: empty list → for loop → if → append."],"rel":["sorting"]},"strseq":{"k":["string methods","split","join","upper","lower","replace","immutable","immutability","cipher","characters"],"s":[["Every letter has an index","A string is a sequence of characters, and — just like a list — each one has an index starting at 0. Your contact sent a clue. Tap letters to spell the code name hidden inside it."],["Slice out the password","Slices work on strings too: msg[start:stop]. Somewhere in each scrambled message a password in capitals is hiding. Move the cut marks to slice it out."],["Strings are sealed","Your code word should be BAT, but it says CAT. With a list you'd just replace item 0… try the same on a string. Then try the agent's way: build a new string."],["The disguise kit","Strings come with handy methods. Each one hands back a disguised copy of the message. Try the kit — and keep an eye on the original. (The ␣ tiles are spaces.)"],["Split and join","split() chops a sentence into a list of words. \"-\".join(words) glues a list back into one string, with your choice of glue between the pieces."],["The cipher wheel","Real spy work: a Caesar cipher shifts every letter along the alphabet — with shift 3, A becomes D. A for loop visits each letter and builds the result. Turn the wheel to decode the message."],["Checkpoint","Two last questions for your agent training. Careful with that second one!"]],"r":["A string is a sequence of characters with indexes from 0: clue[0], clue[-1].","Slices work on strings too: msg[3:8] — stop is not included.","Strings can't be changed in place. word[0] = \"B\" is an error; build a new string instead.","Methods like .upper(), .replace() and .strip() return a new string.",".split() turns a sentence into a list of words; \"-\".join(words) glues them back."],"rel":["strings"]},"tuples":{"k":["tuple","set","unpacking","swap","union","intersection","difference","venn","duplicates","unique"],"s":[["X marks the spot","A tuple is like a list in round brackets: spot = (5, 1). It's perfect for values that belong together — like the x (across) and y (up) of a place on a map. Tap the square each clue points to."],["A sealed parcel","The big difference from a list: a tuple is sealed. Once it's packed you can't change, add or remove anything — Python refuses. Try all three lines."],["Swap the cups","You can unpack a tuple straight into variables: x, y = spot. The same trick swaps two variables in one line. Cup a has juice and cup b has milk — swap them!"],["A bag of unique stickers","A set is a bag that holds each value only once. It's written with curly brackets: {\"⭐\", \"🌙\"}. Add stickers with .add() — and see what happens to a sticker that's already in the bag."],["Venn diagram of friends","Who likes pizza 🍕, who likes ice cream 🍦 — and who likes both? Sets answer these questions with one symbol each. Tap an operator to light up its part of the diagram."],["Remove the duplicates","Turning a list into a set is the quickest way to throw out repeats. Predict the answer, then watch it happen."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions: one about choosing the right collection, one about sets."]],"r":["A tuple is written with round brackets: spot = (5, 1). It has indexes like a list.","Tuples are sealed: you can't change, add or remove items. Make a new tuple instead.","x, y = spot unpacks a tuple; a, b = b, a swaps two variables.","A set {\"⭐\", \"🌙\"} holds each value once and has no order (so no indexes).","Sets combine like a Venn diagram: & both, | either, - only in the first."],"rel":[]},"dicts":{"k":["dictionary","dict","key value","map","mapping","lookup","KeyError","get","items","counting","frequency"],"s":[["Lockers with name tags","A dictionary stores pairs: a key and its value. Think of school lockers — you don't open them by number, you open them by name. Tap a name to look inside."],["New lockers, new stuff","lockers[key] = value does one of two things. If the key is new, a new locker appears. If it already exists, the old item is swapped out. And del removes a locker completely."],["No such locker!","Asking for a key that isn't there crashes your program with a KeyError. There are two safe ways: check with in first, or use .get(), which hands back a default instead of crashing."],["Walk past every locker","A for loop can visit every pair in a dictionary. .items() hands over the key and value together, and we unpack them into two variables — just like a tuple."],["Count the votes","Dictionaries make brilliant counters. Each vote adds 1 to that fruit's count — and .get(fruit, 0) handles fruits nobody has voted for yet. Cast some votes!"],["The emoji translator","Put it all together: a dictionary of words → emoji, split() and a loop. Predict what this program prints — then try the translator yourself."],["Checkpoint","Last two questions of the chapter. Think about keys and values!"]],"r":["A dictionary stores key: value pairs: lockers = {\"ana\": \"🎸\", \"ben\": \"⚽\"}.","lockers[\"ana\"] looks up a value by its key — instantly, no searching.","lockers[\"eli\"] = \"🎨\" adds a new pair, or replaces the value if the key exists. Keys are unique.","A missing key gives a KeyError; lockers.get(\"zed\", \"empty\") returns a default instead.","for name, item in lockers.items(): loops over every pair.","votes[x] = votes.get(x, 0) + 1 is the classic way to count things."],"rel":["nextword","hashing"]},"functions":{"k":["function","def","define","call","reuse","procedure","subroutine"],"s":[["Press the button","Meet the juice machine. Pick a fruit, pull the lever and the machine does all the work. You don't need to know what happens inside — you just use it. Make three different juices."],["Inside the machine","Here is the blueprint that builds the machine. Each part of the code matches a part of the picture. Tap all four parts of the code to see which is which."],["Building is not running","Here's a tiny program with a machine called greet. Read it carefully, top to bottom. What appears on the screen?"],["Follow the juice","Let's watch Python run a program with two calls. At each call it jumps into the machine, runs the body, then jumps back to where it left off. Step through it."],["Write once, use everywhere","Why bother with functions? Compare two ways to serve your guests. Add guests, then change the recipe: everyone now wants ice. Count how many places you'd have to edit!"],["Three ways to break it","Every programmer makes these three mistakes. Run each broken program and see how the machine — and Python — reacts. Try all three."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about building and calling machines."]],"r":["def make_juice(fruit): defines a function — it builds the machine but runs nothing yet.","make_juice(\"🍓\") calls it: Python jumps into the body, runs it, then comes back.","The indented lines are the body. The value in the brackets lands in the parameter fruit.","Write it once, call it many times — change the recipe in one place and every call gets it.","No brackets means no call. Calling before def gives a NameError. A missing value gives a TypeError."],"rel":["potions","lambdas","decorators"]},"params":{"k":["parameter","argument","arguments","return","return value","default argument","keyword argument","kwargs"],"s":[["Fill in the order form","This pizza shop runs on a function with two blanks: size and topping. Fill them in and press Order. Bake three different pizzas."],["Mixed-up orders","Python fills the blanks in order: the first argument goes into the first parameter, and so on. Swap them and the kitchen gets very confused! Then try naming each argument."],["\"The usual, please\"","Most customers want cheese. So the form has a default: topping=\"🧀\" is pre-filled in pencil. If a customer doesn't say a topping, they get the default. Serve the whole queue."],["Shouting vs. handing over","Two chefs work out a price. One prints it — he shouts \"24!\" across the shop. The other returns it — she hands you a box with 24 inside. Then each program tries to add a tip. Run both."],["Follow the box","Watch the values travel. At each call, the arguments fill in a fresh ticket; at return, a box with the answer travels back and lands in the variable on the left of =."],["Can we deliver?","return does one more thing: it ends the function immediately. Our scooter only goes up to 10 km. Slide the house near and far and watch which return runs — and which lines are skipped."],["Checkpoint","Two questions — keep an eye out for print vs return!"]],"r":["Parameters are the blanks in def make_pizza(size, topping):. Arguments are the values you fill in when you call it.","Arguments fill parameters in order — unless you name them: make_pizza(topping=\"🍍\", size=\"large\").","A default value topping=\"🧀\" is used when the caller leaves that argument out.","print only shows a value. return hands it back, so you can store it: bill = price(3).","A function with no return gives back None. return also ends the function straight away."],"rel":["potions"]},"scope":{"k":["local","global","scope","namespace","UnboundLocalError","where variables live"],"s":[["The vanishing cake","A function is like a room. Python bakes a cake in the kitchen, then tries to eat it in the hallway. Run the program — then turn on the fix and run it again."],["Where does Python look?","From inside a room you can still see out the door. When the function uses a name, Python searches: 1 the room, 2 the hallway, 3 its toolbox. Pick a name and watch the search."],["Same name, two notes","There's a snack in the hallway and the kitchen writes snack = ... too. Does the kitchen change the hallway's snack? Predict the last line."],["Room by room","Let's trace a program and watch the two boards. Parameters are notes in the room too. Pay attention to what happens to them when the function returns."],["The scoreboard trap","A game keeps its score in the hallway, and a function tries to add a point. The first version crashes! Run it, then try the two fixes. Score 3 points with a fix."],["Whose note is it?","Time to be the house manager. For each name in this program, decide where its note lives: in the party room (local) or the hallway (global)."],["Checkpoint","Two questions about rooms and hallways."]],"r":["A variable created inside a function is local: it lives in that function's room and is wiped when the function ends.","Variables created outside any function are global — written on the hallway board.","To find a name, Python looks in the room first, then the hallway, then its toolbox of built-ins.","Inside a function, x = ... makes a new local note, even if the hallway has an x too.","To get values in and out, use parameters and return — not global."],"rel":["variables"]},"callstack":{"k":["call stack","stack frame","traceback","RecursionError","stack overflow","nested calls"],"s":[["Who finishes first?","Breakfast can't be served until the toast is ready, and the toast needs butter first. Each job waits for the one it asked for. Which job finishes first?"],["Plates with notes","Each plate carries its function's own variables and a bookmark saying where it paused. Step through a bill that calls a function that calls another function."],["Where was I?","A paused function remembers exactly where it stopped. Read the program, then tap the printed lines in the order they appear."],["The tower that never stops","What if a function calls itself — with nothing to ever stop it? Every call adds a plate, and none of them can finish. Run it and watch the tower."],["Reading a traceback","When a program crashes, Python prints a traceback: a list of the plates that were on the stack at that moment. Tap each entry to find its plate and its line of code."],["Stack builder","Now you write the calls! Choose what soup() and stir() do, then run the kitchen. Two challenges: build a stack 4 plates tall, and then crash it on purpose."],["Checkpoint","Two questions about plates and tracebacks."]],"r":["When a function calls another, the first one pauses and waits.","Python tracks this with the call stack: each call adds a plate on top, with its own local variables.","Only the top plate runs. When it returns, its plate is removed and the one below carries on where it paused.","Last on, first off: the function called last finishes first.","Endless calls pile up plates until Python stops with a RecursionError.","A traceback lists the plates when an error hit — the last entry is where it actually broke."],"rel":["recur","errors"]},"rps":{"k":["project","game","rock paper scissors","random choice","planning","testing","game loop"],"s":[["How the game works","Two players each pick rock, paper or scissors at the same moment. Follow the arrows: each one beats the one it points at. Judge four matches."],["Plan before you code","Programmers don't write a whole game in one go. They split it into small jobs, and each job becomes a function with a name that says what it does. Match each job to its function."],["Getting the two moves","The computer picks with random.choice(), which grabs a random item from a list. The player types — and we must keep asking until they type a real move. Try both!"],["Test the referee","The winner() function is the referee. A dictionary holds the rules: BEATS[\"rock\"] is \"scissors\". Test every possible match by tapping each square of the table."],["Bug hunt","A friend wrote their own winner(). It prints player when you win… but the score never goes up. Look at the code and the output. What's the bug?"],["Play the real game!","Everything is connected. Each round, play_round() calls the other functions — watch the code and the call stack behind the scenes while you play a 5-round match."],["Final checkpoint","The last two questions of the chapter. Use the BEATS dictionary and the winner() function."]],"r":["Big programs are built from small functions, each with one clear job — planning that is called decomposition.","random.choice(MOVES) picks a random item from a list.","A while move not in MOVES: loop keeps asking until the input is valid.","A dictionary of rules (BEATS) keeps winner() short — and testing every case catches mistakes.","Functions that work something out must return it; printing isn't enough."],"rel":[]},"errors":{"k":["error","error message","exception","debug","debugging","traceback","SyntaxError","NameError","TypeError","bug"],"s":[["The crime scene","A little shop program has crashed. Don't panic: the red message Python prints is a set of clues. Run it, then tap the three clues to fill in the detective's notebook."],["The usual suspects","Most crashes are caused by the same six suspects. Each error's name tells you which one did it. Read each case and pick the suspect."],["Follow the footprints","Python points at the line where it noticed a problem. But sometimes that line is innocent, and the real culprit struck earlier. Where is the mistake here?"],["The silent bug","This program should print the average score: 8.0. It prints something else, and there's no error message at all! Shine a torch inside the loop with a print(), then fix it."],["Close the case","Four broken programs, four error messages. Read each message carefully — it often tells you exactly what to change — then pick the fix."],["Crash lab","Detectives also stage crashes on purpose to learn how they happen. Choose what the user types, run the program, and watch where it stops. Can you make it crash in three different ways?"],["Checkpoint","Two questions to prove you're a real code detective."]],"r":["An error message has three clues: where (file and line), what kind (like NameError) and the details.","Read it from the bottom up: the last line says what went wrong.","The usual suspects: SyntaxError, NameError, TypeError, ValueError, IndexError and KeyError.","The line Python points at is where it noticed the problem. The real cause can be on an earlier line.","Some bugs give no error at all, just a wrong answer. Add print() lines to see what your variables really hold.","Python stops at the first error: the lines after it never run."],"rel":["callstack"]},"tryexcept":{"k":["try","except","exception handling","catch","error handling","finally","ValueError","validation"],"s":[["The show must go on","The acrobat does as many flips as the audience shouts. But what if someone shouts a word instead of a number? Try both, with and without the safety net."],["Parts of the act","Every safety net has the same parts. Tap each highlighted line of the code to see which part of the circus it is."],["Mid-act jump","What happens to the rest of the act after a slip? Read the program and predict exactly what it prints."],["Ask until it works","A real program shouldn't give up after one bad answer. This one keeps asking until it gets a number. Step through it while the user types ten, then 9."],["The right net","A net only catches the kind of fall written on its label. Share 8 pizza slices between the guests and test every answer. Can you make all three safe?"],["Applause and a bow","Two more optional parts: else: is the applause, it only happens if nobody fell. finally: is the bow, it happens every time. Predict what's printed for each answer."],["Checkpoint","Two questions about nets and falls."]],"r":["Put risky lines inside try:, and what to do if they fail inside except SomeError:.","If a line in try fails, Python jumps straight to the matching except. The rest of the try block is skipped.","After an except block, the program carries on instead of crashing.","An except only catches the error type it names. Name the errors you expect, so real bugs still show up.","while True: with try, break and except keeps asking until the input works.","else: runs only if nothing failed. finally: runs every time."],"rel":[]},"search":{"k":["linear search","binary search","find","lookup","algorithm","halving","divide and conquer"],"s":[["Find book #42","The librarian needs book #42, but this shelf is a mess: the books are in no order at all. You can only read a number by pulling the book out. Tap books to find it."],["One by one, in code","Here is the same plan written as a function. It walks along the shelf with an index i and returns the position as soon as it finds the book. Step through it."],["The sorted shelf","Good news: this shelf is sorted, smallest to biggest. Now every book you open tells you which way to go, so you can rule out a whole chunk of the shelf at once. Find each book in 5 checks or fewer."],["Halving, in code","A binary search remembers the part of the shelf that's still possible with two numbers: low and high. Each round it opens the middle book, mid, and moves one end. Step through a search for 23."],["The phone book trick","A giant phone book lists 1,000,000 names in alphabetical order. You open it in the middle, throw away the half that can't contain the name, and repeat. How many checks until you find any name?"],["Only on a sorted shelf","Someone used binary search on a messy shelf. Book #63 is right there… so what could go wrong? Run the search and watch."],["Checkpoint","Two questions about finding things fast."]],"r":["To search a list that isn't in order, check the items one by one: a linear search. With n items it can take n checks.","If the list is sorted, open the middle and throw away half every time: a binary search.","Halving is amazingly fast: 1,000,000 items need at most 20 checks.","Binary search uses low, high and mid to remember which part is still possible.","Binary search only works on sorted data. On a messy list it can miss items that are there.","A search usually returns the index it found, or -1 for \"not found\"."],"rel":["curves","trees"]},"sorting":{"k":["sort","sorting algorithm","bubble sort","selection sort","order","key","reverse"],"s":[["Class photo","The photographer wants everyone lined up shortest to tallest, left to right. The rule: you can only swap two kids standing next to each other. Tap ⇄ to swap them."],["Bubble sort","Here's a recipe a computer can follow: walk along the line comparing neighbours, and swap them if the left one is taller. You be the computer for the first pass."],["Bubble sort, in code","Two loops: the outer loop does one pass at a time, the inner loop walks along comparing neighbours i and i + 1. Step through it with four kids."],["Pick the shortest","A different recipe: find the shortest kid still waiting and send them to the next spot. Tap the shortest kid in the waiting part each time."],["Let Python do it","In real programs you don't write your own sort: Python has a fast one built in. sorted() gives back a new sorted list, and two extras change the order. Try all four lines."],["Capitals jump the queue","Three name tags: bob, Zed and amy. Surely sorted() puts them in alphabetical order… Predict the output."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions to lock in what you've sorted out."]],"r":["Sorting means putting things in order by comparing two at a time and swapping them when they're the wrong way round.","Bubble sort compares neighbours along the line. Each pass carries the biggest item to the end.","Selection sort finds the smallest of what's left and puts it next in line.","In real programs use sorted(my_list): it gives back a new sorted list. reverse=True flips the order.","key= sorts by something else, like key=len to sort words by their length.","Capital letters come before lowercase ones. Use key=str.lower to ignore capitals."],"rel":["listwork","lambdas"]},"bigo":{"k":["big o","big-o","complexity","time complexity","efficiency","performance","speed","linear","quadratic","logarithmic","constant time","scaling"],"s":[["The party grows","At this party, the host says hi to every guest, and every guest shakes hands with every other guest. Keep inviting guests and watch both numbers."],["Counting steps","A stopwatch isn't fair: some computers are faster than others. Instead, programmers count steps. For a party of 10 guests, how many steps does each job take?"],["Double the party","The party grows from 1,000 to 2,000 guests. How much more work does each job need? Predict all four."],["The race","Now let's race the four jobs on a real computer doing 10 million steps per second. Pick the size of the party and press Race! Try at least three sizes, including a million guests."],["Spot the speed","Can you tell how fast code is just by reading it? Drop each snippet into the right box. Look for loops, loops inside loops, and halving."],["Make it faster","Did anyone sign up twice? The slow way compares every pair of names. The fast way remembers names it has seen in a set. Can you check a million guests in under a second?"],["Checkpoint","Two questions about growing parties, and Chapter 6 is complete!"]],"r":["We measure how fast code is by counting steps as the input grows, not with a stopwatch.","O(1), constant: the same steps however big the input, like guests[0].","O(log n), logarithmic: halving. Doubling the input adds just one step.","O(n), linear: one step per item. Double the input, double the work.","O(n²), quadratic: every item paired with every other item. Double the input, 4× the work.","A smarter idea, like remembering what you've seen in a set, can turn hours into a fraction of a second."],"rel":["curves","hashing"]},"recur":{"k":["recursion","recursive","recursive function","base case","self-calling","factorial","russian dolls","divide and conquer"],"s":[["Dolls inside dolls","This wooden doll has a secret: open it and there's another doll inside, just smaller. Keep opening until you find the one that can't be opened."],["A function that calls itself","Here is the doll job written in Python. Look at line 6: inside open_doll, the function calls itself. Tap the three parts to see what each one does."],["Down and back up","Now the function will give back an answer: how many dolls are there? Each doll says \"me, plus all the dolls inside me\". Step through it and watch the answers travel back up."],["Every doll has its own size","Here's the tricky part: every call to count gets its own brand-new box called size. Same name, but a different box for each doll. We've paused while all 4 calls are open. Tap each doll to look inside its box."],["Two ways to break it","Recursion goes wrong in two classic ways. Pick a version of count, run it and watch the dolls. Try all three."],["Before or after?","This function prints each doll's size. Run it, then answer a question: what happens if print comes after the call to itself instead of before?"],["Your turn: sweets","Each doll hides as many sweets as its size: doll 4 holds 4, doll 3 holds 3… Finish sweets(size) so it adds up the sweets in all the dolls. Pick the two missing lines, then run the tests."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about functions that call themselves."]],"r":["A recursive function solves a problem by calling itself on a smaller piece of the same problem.","The base case is the smallest piece, answered straight away with no more calls. It's what stops the recursion.","The recursive case calls the function again with a smaller input, so every call gets closer to the base case.","Calls go down (each one waits), the base case answers, then the answers travel back up to finish each waiting call.","Every call gets its own variables: four calls to count means four separate size boxes. A finished call's box is thrown away.","No base case, or steps that don't get smaller, means calls never end: RecursionError.","Code before the recursive call runs on the way down; code after it runs on the way back up."],"rel":["callstack","trees","memo"]},"fractals":{"k":["fractal","recursive drawing","tree","snowflake","koch","turtle","depth first","self-similar"],"s":[["Grow a tree","This tree is drawn by one tiny rule: a branch, with two smaller trees on top. Drag the slider to grow it one level at a time."],["A tree on every branch","Tap any branch: everything growing from it lights up. It's a complete tree, just smaller. That's why the code only needs to draw one branch and then call itself twice."],["Which side first?","A turtle draws the tree one line at a time. At every fork, does it work on both sides a bit at a time, or finish one whole side first? Predict, then watch."],["Design your own tree","Same code, different numbers. Change the angle, the shrink and the depth until your tree matches the faint grey target."],["How many branches?","Every new level puts two branches on each tip. Fill in the missing totals: how fast does the tree grow?"],["A snowflake from a line","A different rule: swap a straight line for four shorter lines with a bump in the middle. Then do the same to each of those lines, and again… Turn up the depth!"],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about drawing with recursion."]],"r":["A fractal is a shape made of smaller copies of itself, like a tree made of smaller trees.","A tree function draws one branch, then calls itself twice: once for the left tree and once for the right.","Its base case is depth == 0: draw nothing. Each call uses depth - 1, so it always gets there.","Each call finishes completely before the next line runs, so the whole left tree is drawn before the right one (depth-first).","Two calls per level means the branches double every level: 1, 3, 7, 15… depth 10 already has 1,023.","The Koch rule swaps each straight line for 4 smaller ones with a bump. Repeat it and a triangle becomes a snowflake."],"rel":["trees"]},"modules":{"k":["module","import","library","package","from import","math module","standard library"],"s":[["Borrow, don't build","You have three jobs to do. You could write all the code yourself, or borrow a tool from Python's shed. For each job, tap the toolbox that has the right tool."],["Reaching into the box","import math puts the math box on your workbench. To use a tool, write the box name, a dot, then the tool. Try all four tools."],["Two ways to get it wrong","Python only knows about the boxes you import, and it only looks inside a box when you name it. Run all three versions and watch the workbench."],["Just one tool, please","Sometimes you only need one tool. from random import choice brings just that tool to your bench, without the box. Run both versions, then answer the question."],["Fix the imports","Three programs, three crashes. Read the last line of each error like a detective, then pick the fix. One of them uses a nickname: import random as r."],["Build your own toolbox","Any Python file can be a toolbox! main.py imports a file called party.py. Put the right functions into party.py so that main.py runs without crashing."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about borrowing tools."]],"r":["A module is a toolbox of ready-made code. Python comes with hundreds: math, random, time…","import math brings the whole box. Reach inside with a dot: math.sqrt(81), math.pi.","Forget the import and you get NameError: name 'math' is not defined.","from random import choice brings just one tool, so you use it without the box name: choice(snacks).","import random as r gives the box a nickname: r.randint(1, 6). A misspelled box gives ModuleNotFoundError.","Any .py file is a module too: party.py next to your program can be used with import party."],"rel":[]},"files":{"k":["file","read file","write file","open","with","save","FileNotFoundError","text file"],"s":[["A memory that forgets","Click the button a few times, then close the program and start it again. Where did your clicks go? Then switch on Save to a file and try again."],["Open, write, close","open() takes the notebook off the shelf and hands you a pen called f. f.write() writes, and f.close() puts it back, saved. Step through it and watch the page."],["\"w\" wipes, \"a\" adds","The diary already has one line in it. This program opens it with \"w\" and writes day 2. Predict what the diary will say, then try both modes."],["Reading it back","Now let's read the shopping list. Try three ways of reading it, and keep an eye on the line box: something is hiding at the end of every line!"],["with: it closes itself","Forgetting close() is easy. So Python has with: the notebook stays open for the indented block, then closes by itself. Step through it."],["Project: the high score","A dice game that remembers your best score in best.txt. The very first time, the file doesn't exist yet, and try/except catches the FileNotFoundError. Play a few rounds!"],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about files."]],"r":["Variables live in memory and vanish when the program ends. A file stays on the disk.","open(\"diary.txt\", \"w\") opens a file for writing; .write(text) adds text; .close() saves and closes it.","\\n means new line. .write() doesn't add one for you.","Mode \"w\" starts a fresh page (it wipes the file!). Mode \"a\" adds to the end. Mode \"r\" (the default) reads.",".read() gives the whole file as one string. for line in f: gives one line at a time, each still ending in \\n, so use .strip().","with open(...) as f: closes the file for you. Opening a missing file to read gives FileNotFoundError, which you can catch with try/except."],"rel":["dataformats"]},"comps":{"k":["list comprehension","comprehension","one-liner","filter","transform","clean data"],"s":[["The doubling machine","Numbers ride along a conveyor belt into a machine that doubles them. Run the belt, then switch to the one-liner and run it again. Do they make the same list?"],["Reading the one-liner","A list comprehension has three parts, and each one is a piece of the factory. Tap each part to see which piece it is."],["Swap the machine","Same belt of words, different machines. Pick a machine, run the belt, and try to make both target lists."],["Add a gate","Put an if at the end, and a gate appears on the belt: only items that pass the test get to the machine. The rest drop into the bin. Make both targets."],["From loop to one-liner","Turn each loop into a comprehension by tapping the pieces in the right order. Careful: the order inside the brackets matters, and one piece doesn't belong!"],["Clean the guest list","People typed their names into a party form, and it's a mess: extra spaces, CAPITALS and empty entries. Choose a machine and a gate to turn it into ['Ana', 'Ben', 'Cara']."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about one-line factories."]],"r":["A list comprehension builds a new list in one line: [n * 2 for n in nums].","Read it as \"n times 2, for each n in nums\". The part before for is the machine; the for part is the belt; the brackets are the basket.","Add a gate at the end to keep only some items: [n for n in nums if n > 3].","The order is always: what to make → for … in … → optional if.","It does exactly what a for loop with .append() does, just shorter."],"rel":["lists","loops"]},"classes":{"k":["class","object","oop","object oriented","instance","constructor","__init__","self","attribute"],"s":[["One cutter, many cookies","You have one star-shaped cookie cutter. Pick a flavour and some sprinkles, then press the cutter. Make three cookies."],["Inside the cutter","Here's the cutter written in Python. Tap the four highlighted parts to find out what each one does."],["Who is self?","Step through making two cookies. Watch the self badge: it always sits on the cookie that's being decorated right now."],["Every cookie is its own","Two cookies came from the same cutter. If you change one, does the other change too? Predict, then run the lines and watch."],["Fix the bakery bugs","Three programs, three crashes. Read the last line of each error, then pick the fix."],["Bake an order","A customer orders 3 cookies, all different flavours, with exactly 12 sprinkles in total. Bake them onto the tray (tap a cookie to throw it away), then deliver."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about cutters and cookies."]],"r":["A class is a plan (the cookie cutter). An object is one thing made from it (a cookie).","Calling the class, like Cookie(\"choc\", 5), makes a new object and runs __init__ on it.","Inside __init__, self means the object being made right now.","self.flavor = flavor stores an attribute on that object. Read it later with a dot: c1.flavor.","Every object keeps its own attributes: changing c1 never changes c2.","Asking for an attribute that isn't there gives AttributeError. Leaving out an argument gives TypeError."],"rel":["dunder"]},"methods":{"k":["method","self","object methods","behaviour","oop"],"s":[["Meet Pip","Pip is a little virtual pet who gets hungry and bored as time passes. Feed and play with Pip until it's happy (80+) and full (hunger 20 or less) at the same time."],["A function that belongs","Here's how feed is written inside the Pet class. Tap the three parts. The last one shows Python's little secret about self."],["Two pets, one class","Rex and Bella are both made from Pet. Call some methods on each of them and watch where the self badge lands."],["Asking the pet a question","play takes an extra number: how many minutes to play. And mood() doesn't change Rex at all: it returns an answer. Get print(rex.mood()) to show 😄 happy."],["Where did self go?","Forgetting self is the most common mistake with methods. Read each error, then pick the fix."],["Plan Rex's day","Plan 4 activities for Rex. After each one an hour passes, and Rex gets 10 hungrier. Make Rex end the day 😄 happy: happy 80+, without getting too hungry (over 60)."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about methods and self."]],"r":["A method is a function that lives inside a class: def feed(self):. Every object of that class can use it.","Call it on an object with a dot: rex.feed(). Python turns this into Pet.feed(rex).","So self is the object before the dot, and self.hunger is that object's hunger.","Methods can take extra parameters (play(self, minutes)) and return answers (mood(self)).","Forget self in the def and you get a TypeError; forget self. in front of an attribute and Python looks for a plain variable instead."],"rel":[]},"inherit":{"k":["inheritance","subclass","parent class","child class","override","super","isinstance","polymorphism","oop"],"s":[["The animal family","Every animal can eat and speak, so that's written once, in Animal. Dogs, cats and birds are children of Animal. Tap each child to see everything it can do."],["Look up the tree","rex is a Dog and tom is a Cat. When you call a method, Python looks in the object's own class first, then climbs up to the parent. Try all four calls."],["Grandchildren","A Puppy is a child of Dog, which is a child of Animal. Puppies have their own speak(). Predict what bo.eat() gives, then watch the search."],["super(): don't skip the parent","Dogs need an extra attribute: a trick. So Dog writes its own __init__, which replaces Animal's. Run it with and without the super() line."],["Is it a kind of…?","isinstance(thing, Class) asks: \"is this thing a kind of that class?\" Remember the family tree, and decide True or False for each one."],["The zoo concert","Add two new kinds of animal to the family, each with its own speak(). Then start the concert: one loop asks every animal to speak."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about family trees of classes."]],"r":["class Dog(Animal): makes Dog a child of Animal: a Dog gets everything an Animal has.","A child can add new methods (fetch()) and override ones it inherits (its own speak()).","Calling a method looks in the object's own class first, then walks up the tree. Not found anywhere → AttributeError.","If a child writes its own __init__, call super().__init__(…) so the parent's setup still runs.","isinstance(rex, Animal) is True for a Dog: a Dog is a kind of Animal.","One loop can call a.speak() on many kinds of animals, and each answers its own way."],"rel":[]},"dunder":{"k":["dunder","magic methods","special methods","__str__","__add__","__eq__","operator overloading","vector class"],"s":[["An ugly print","A Vec is a move on a treasure map: Vec(2, 1) means 2 steps right, 1 step up. Let's print one. Run it without and then with the __str__ method."],["Adding arrows","Moves join tip to tail: do v1, then v2 from where you landed. Try v1 + v2 without __add__ first, then add it and land exactly on the 💰."],["Python's secret translations","Every symbol you use is secretly a method call. Tap something you write, then tap what Python really runs. Match all four."],["Equal… or the same?","a and b are two separate Vecs with the same numbers. Is a == b True? Predict, then run it without and with __eq__."],["Stretch it","__mul__ makes v * n stretch an arrow n times longer. Pick the right number to reach each treasure."],["Treasure hunt","The treasure is at (7, 5). Add exactly three move cards to pos, one at a time, to land on it. Each tap runs pos = pos + move."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about special methods."]],"r":["Methods with double underscores, like __str__, are special methods: Python calls them for you.","print(v) calls v.__str__(). Without it you get something like <__main__.Vec object at 0x…>.","a + b runs a.__add__(b). Without __add__, it's a TypeError: unsupported operand type(s).","Without __eq__, a == b only checks whether they're the very same object. Define it to compare the values.","a * 3 runs a.__mul__(3). Special methods let your own objects use Python's ordinary symbols."],"rel":["vecadd","classes"]},"stacks":{"k":["stack","queue","lifo","fifo","deque","undo","push","pop","brackets","data structure"],"s":[["A stack of pancakes","You can only put a pancake on top, and only eat the one on top. Make the stack match the order card, from the bottom up."],["The undo button","Every word you type is pushed onto a history stack. Undo pops the newest word off. Oops, someone typed I hate broccoli. Change it to I love pizza."],["The ice-cream line","A line works the other way round: new people join at the back, and the shop serves the front. Let people arrive and serve 3 customers."],["Stack or queue?","Stacks and queues are everywhere once you start looking. For each one, ask: who goes first, the newest or the oldest?"],["Don't make everyone shuffle","When the front person leaves a list with pop(0), everyone behind them steps forward one place. A deque (say \"deck\") just remembers where the front is. Try both ways."],["The bracket checker","Code editors use a stack to check brackets. Every opener is pushed; every closer must match the opener on top, which is popped. Guess each one, then run the checker."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about stacks and queues."]],"r":["A stack is last in, first out: you only add to and take from the top. A list does it with append() and pop().","The undo button is a stack: every action is pushed, and Undo pops the most recent one.","A queue is first in, first out: join at the back, get served at the front.","list.pop(0) works as a queue, but every other item has to shuffle forward. deque with popleft() doesn't shuffle anything.","Popping from an empty list gives IndexError: pop from empty list.","A stack can check brackets: push every opener, and every closer must match the one on top."],"rel":["lists"]},"refs":{"k":["reference","mutable","immutable","mutability","copy","aliasing","pointer","same object","data structure"],"s":[["The shared shopping list","Mum writes a shopping list. Dad says \"I'll use that one too\" and adds eggs. What does Mum's list say now?"],["Same balloon, or just the same look?","Step through the code. Watch the tags and balloons: c looks the same as a, but is it the same balloon?"],["Making a real copy","You want to try changes on a list without wrecking the original. Run the program both ways and see which one leaves team alone."],["Numbers can't change","These two programs look alike, but one changes the original and one doesn't. Run both and watch what happens to the balloons."],["Functions get a tag too","When you pass a list to a function, its parameter is just another tag on your list. Run both versions of add_star. Which one changes mine?"],["The haunted grid","Make a 3×3 grid of zeros and paint the diagonal (top-left to bottom-right) with 1s. Tap cells to set them. But something spooky happens with one way of building the grid…"],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about names and values."]],"r":["A variable name is a tag tied to a value. The value (the balloon) lives on its own.","b = a doesn't copy anything: it ties a second tag to the same list. Change it through b and a sees it too.","Make a real, separate list with a.copy() (or a[:] or list(a)).","== asks \"do they look the same?\"; is asks \"is it the very same balloon?\".","Numbers, strings and tuples can't change. y = y + 1 makes a new balloon and moves the tag.","A function gets a tag on your list, so append inside it changes your list.","[[0] * 3] * 3 makes three tags on one row. Build grids with a comprehension instead."],"rel":["variables","lists"]},"trees":{"k":["tree","node","root","leaf","binary tree","binary search tree","bst","traversal","hierarchy","folders","data structure"],"s":[["Folders inside folders","Your teacher wants your drawing.png, but where did you save it? Open folders to find it."],["Turn it upside down","Here are the same folders, drawn as a tree: computer trees grow downwards, with the root at the top. Tap the four words to learn the names of the parts."],["A tree in Python","Each node is a Node object with a name and a list of children. Tap a node to pick it as the parent, then tap a name to add it as a child. Build the tree on the card."],["Visiting every node","show prints a node, then calls itself on each child, one step deeper. Before you run it: after photos, what gets printed next?"],["How big is each folder?","A folder's size is the sizes of everything inside it, added up. Your disk is too full: tap files to delete them until home is exactly 12 MB."],["A tree for searching","In a binary search tree, every node has at most two children: smaller numbers go to the left, bigger ones to the right. Walk each new number down to its spot."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about trees."]],"r":["A tree starts at one root. Each node can have children; a node with no children is a leaf.","A tiny Node class with a children list is enough to build any tree: home.children.append(pics).","Trees are made of smaller trees, so recursion fits them perfectly: do the node, then call yourself on each child.","To add up a tree, each folder asks its children for their totals and adds them together.","In a binary search tree smaller values go left and bigger ones go right, so finding a value takes only a few checks."],"rel":["recur","fractals","search","graphs"]},"graphs":{"k":["graph","network","nodes and edges","bfs","breadth first search","shortest path","maze","adjacency list","pathfinding","data structure"],"s":[["Places and roads","You're at home and want to visit the farm. Tap places one by one along the roads. Can you get there using the fewest roads?"],["A dict of neighbours","In Python a graph is usually a dict: each place maps to the list of places one road away. Tap 3 places to see their lists."],["Spreading the news","Big news at home! Each minute, everyone who just heard it phones their neighbours. Python does this with a queue: whoever heard first passes it on first. Press Next to follow it."],["Why remember who heard?","What if we forget the heard set and just phone every neighbour each time? Run both versions and watch the queue."],["The shortest route","Add one thing to the search: whenever a place hears the news, write down who told it in came_from. The arrows show it. Tap 3 places to find the shortest route there from home."],["Solve the maze","A maze is a graph too: each square is a node, joined to the open squares beside it. The mouse always takes the shortest path to the cheese. Place 2 boxes so its path becomes at least 14 steps, without trapping it."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about graphs."]],"r":["A graph is a set of nodes (places) joined by edges (roads). Unlike a tree, it can have loops.","Store it as a dict of neighbours: roads[\"home\"] is the list of places one road away.","Breadth-first search spreads out like news: a queue of places to visit, and a heard set so nobody is visited twice.","Without the visited set, the search walks round in circles forever.","Remember who told whom (came_from), then follow it backwards to get the shortest route.","A maze is a graph too: each square is a node, joined to the open squares next to it."],"rel":["trees"]},"hashing":{"k":["hash","hash table","hash map","hashmap","hash function","collision","dictionary internals","data structure"],"s":[["Where is Zoe's coat?","Zoe wants her coat back, but the coats are in a big pile. Tap coats to check their name tags until you find Zoe's."],["Turning a name into a number","How does the coat check pick a hook? A hash function turns the name into a number. Ours adds up the letters (a = 1, b = 2, …) and keeps the remainder after dividing by 8. Hash 3 names."],["Hang it up, find it fast","A dict is a coat check: coats[\"Ana\"] = \"🧥\" hashes \"Ana\" and hangs the coat on that hook. Hang all 4 coats, then find one."],["Two coats, one hook","Sometimes two names hash to the same hook. That's called a collision. Hang the three new coats and see what the hook does."],["Keys that can't change","Imagine hanging a coat by a name, then changing the name. Its hook number would change, and the coat would be lost! So Python only allows keys that can't change. Which of these can be dict keys?"],["Who signed in twice?","Someone signed the party guest book twice. The program remembers every name it has seen. Run it with seen as a list, then as a set, and compare the number of checks."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about hash tables."]],"r":["Searching a pile (a list) means checking items one by one. Lots of items, lots of checks.","A hash function turns a key into a number. The same key always gives the same number.","A hash table uses that number as a hook (slot), so finding a key is about one look, however many there are.","Two keys can get the same hook: a collision. The hook keeps a short list, and Python keeps most hooks empty so lists stay short.","Dict keys and set items must be unchangeable (numbers, strings, tuples). A list key gives TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'.","x in my_set and my_dict[key] are fast; x in my_list checks every item."],"rel":["dicts","bigo"]},"memo":{"k":["memoization","memoisation","cache","caching","dynamic programming","dp","fibonacci","lru_cache","stairs"],"s":[["Up the stairs","Kit the cat climbs 4 stairs, taking 1 or 2 steps at a time. 1+1+2 is one way; 2+2 is another. Find all the different ways."],["Look at the last move","Here's the trick. To stand on stair n, Kit's last move came from stair n − 1 (a 1-step) or stair n − 2 (a 2-step). So the ways add up. Tap each ? to fill in the table."],["Déjà vu","Here's the code, and every call it makes for ways(5). Each call asks two smaller ones. Tap the calls: some of them get worked out more than once. Find the one repeated the most."],["The explosion","Each extra stair almost doubles the number of calls. Drag the slider and watch. Get to 35 stairs or more."],["Keep a notebook","The fix: keep a memo notebook (a dict). Before working an answer out, check the notebook; after working it out, write it down. Run ways(6) both ways and count the calls."],["Let Python keep the notebook","Memoizing is so useful that Python has it built in: put @cache on the line above the def. Try ways(40) without it, then with it."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about memoization."]],"r":["To climb n stairs, your last move was a 1-step or a 2-step, so ways(n) = ways(n - 1) + ways(n - 2).","The plain recursive version works out the same smaller answers again and again, so its calls explode as n grows.","Memoization: keep a notebook (a dict memo). Check it first; after working an answer out, write it down.","With a memo, each ways(k) is worked out once: about n calls instead of hundreds of millions.","from functools import cache and @cache above the def add the notebook for you."],"rel":["recur"]},"lambdas":{"k":["lambda","anonymous function","map","filter","functional programming","key function","pipeline"],"s":[["A rule on a sticky note","Robo is a market helper. Give it one sticky note with a rule, and it uses that rule on every crate's price. The boss wants the new prices to be 4, 6, 9, 3. Which note?"],["Inside the sticky note","A lambda is a short way to write a small function. Both of these do exactly the same job. Tap the four parts of the lambda."],["Write the rule","Each round shows what the rule must turn 1, 2, 3, 4 into. Pick the answer part of lambda n: … that makes the bottom row match."],["Keep what passes","filter() asks the rule a yes/no question about each crate and keeps only the yes ones. A shopper wants only the fruit that costs less than 5: keep [3, 2, 4]."],["Line them up","sorted() can take a key= rule: it sorts by the rule's answer instead of by the item itself. Pick the key that gives each target order."],["The sale pipeline","Big sale: everything under 10 is half price. What do the cheap items cost altogether after the discount? Put the two steps in order, then run. The answer should be 8.0."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about lambda, map and filter."]],"r":["lambda x: x * 2 is a tiny function with no name. The part after the colon is returned automatically.","map(rule, items) applies the rule to every item. Wrap it in list() to see the results.","filter(rule, items) keeps only the items where the rule gives True.","sorted(items, key=rule) lines items up by the rule's answer, like key=len for word length.","You can chain them: filter first, then map, then sum. The order matters!","A list comprehension can do the same jobs: pick whichever reads more clearly."],"rel":["functions","sorting"]},"generators":{"k":["generator","iterator","yield","next","iter","lazy","lazy evaluation","StopIteration","memory"],"s":[["You only wanted three","You want 3 gumballs. One machine insists on making all a million first. The other makes one each time you turn the crank. Try both."],["Next, next, next…","iter() puts a bookmark at the start of a list. Each next() hands you the item at the bookmark and moves it on. Keep pressing next(it): what happens when you run out?"],["yield: hand it out and pause","A generator function uses yield instead of return. Each next() runs it until the next yield, hands out that value, and then the function pauses on that line, remembering n. Press next(c) until it runs out."],["Nothing happens… yet","A sneaky one. What does this program print when g = gen() runs?"],["The endless machine","This machine never runs out: its loop is while True. A list could never hold it, but a generator is fine because it only makes what you ask for. Crank until you have 3 red gumballs."],["Brackets that save memory","To add up a million squares, you don't need them all at once. Compare the memory for square brackets (a list) and round brackets (a generator). Try both with a million or more."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about generators."]],"r":["A list makes every item up front. A generator makes items one at a time, only when asked.","next(it) asks for the next item. When there are none left, Python raises StopIteration.","A for loop secretly calls next() for you, and stops quietly at StopIteration.","yield hands back a value and pauses the function right there, keeping its variables, until the next next().","Calling a generator function runs none of its code yet; the first next() starts it.","Generators can go on forever (while True: yield …): just take what you need and break.","A generator expression (x * x for x in …) uses almost no memory, even for millions of items."],"rel":["loops"]},"decorators":{"k":["decorator","wrapper","@","higher order function","functions as values","closure"],"s":[["Hand over a function","A function is a value, just like a number. You can hand one to another function. twice takes any function and calls it two times. Try giving it different functions."],["Wrapping paper","wrap takes a function and builds a new function, wrapper, that does something before and after it, like wrapping paper around a toy. Step through: notice when things actually get printed."],["The @ shortcut","Usually we want the toy itself to come wrapped, so we write toy = wrap(toy). Python has a shortcut for exactly that: @wrap. Run both versions."],["Let things through","Our wrapper only works for toys with no inputs and no answer. Wrap add(a, b) and two things break. Read what goes wrong, then pick the fix."],["Handy decorators","Here are three ready-made decorators. Put one above greet to make the output match each target card."],["Layers of paper","You can stack decorators. @exclaim adds a \"!\", and @repeat says the answer 3 times. Put them in the two slots so that greet() gives hi! hi! hi!"],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about decorators."]],"r":["Functions are values: you can pass one to another function, like twice(cheer), without the brackets.","A decorator is a function that takes a function and returns a new wrapper function that does extra work before and after.","@wrap above a def is just a short way of writing toy = wrap(toy).","Real wrappers take *args and return f(*args), so arguments and answers pass straight through.","Stacked decorators wrap from the bottom up: the one nearest the def goes on first, the top one is outermost."],"rel":["functions"]},"dataformats":{"k":["json","csv","data file","parse","load data","save data","DictReader","serialisation"],"s":[["Just text with commas","The school weather station saves its readings as weather.csv. Open it and it's just text! Tap each line to split it at the commas."],["Rows become dicts","Python's csv module does the splitting for you. DictReader turns every row into a dict, using the header for the keys. Look closely at the printed row, then answer."],["Ask the data","Time to answer two questions about the week. For each one, pick the line of code that gets it right. Watch out for strings!"],["JSON looks like Python","Games often keep their save files as JSON. It looks almost exactly like a Python dict! Tap the two words that Python writes differently."],["Save your game","Play a little, then Save, Quit (memory is wiped!) and Load. Your progress should come back from the file."],["Messy data","On Sunday the thermometer broke, so the station saved an empty temperature. Now the average-temperature program crashes. Pick the fix that gives the right average."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about data files."]],"r":["A CSV file is plain text: one row per line, commas between the columns, and usually a header line first.","csv.DictReader turns each row into a dict keyed by the header: row[\"temp\"].","Everything read from a CSV is a string: use int() or float() before doing maths, or comparisons go wrong.","JSON looks almost like Python: true/false/null become True/False/None.","json.dump(data, f) saves dicts and lists to a file; json.load(f) brings them back.","Real data is messy: check for empty values before converting them."],"rel":["files"]},"numpy":{"k":["numpy","array","ndarray","matrix","image","pixels","vectorised","mask","data science"],"s":[["A photo is numbers","Here's a tiny photo of a cat. Zoom in all the way and see what the computer really stores."],["Lists vs arrays","A Python list and a NumPy array both hold 1, 2, 3. Try * 2 and + 10 on both and compare."],["Edit the whole photo","Our photo is an array called img. One line of maths changes all 100 pixels at once. Pick the line that makes each target picture."],["Find a pixel","img.shape is (10, 10): 10 rows and 10 columns. img[row, col] picks one pixel, row first. Tap the pixel each line asks for."],["Crop with slices","Slices work on grids too: img[rows, cols]. Set the four numbers so the crop matches the dashed box. Careful: the stop number is not included!"],["A grid of True and False","Comparing an array checks every pixel: img > 185 makes a new grid of True and False called a mask. Slide the number until the mask finds just the 8 eye pixels."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about arrays."]],"r":["A black-and-white photo is a grid of numbers: 0 is black, 255 is white.","np.array(...) makes an array. Maths happens to every item at once: arr * 2, img + 50, 255 - img.","A list repeats with * 2 and refuses + 10; an array does the maths instead.","img.shape gives (rows, columns), and img[row, col] picks one pixel: row first.","img[4:6, 2:4] crops rows and columns. The stop number is not included.","img > 185 makes a grid of True/False (a mask), and mask.sum() counts the Trues."],"rel":["bigvectors","matrix"]},"pandas":{"k":["pandas","dataframe","table","spreadsheet","groupby","filter rows","data analysis","data science"],"s":[["A table in Python","The bake-sale teams wrote down every snack they sold. pandas loads the file as a DataFrame: a table Python can work with. Tap the part of the table each question asks for."],["A new column","How much money did each snack make? Multiply two columns and pandas does it row by row, for every row at once. Run the line, then answer."],["Filter the rows","A filter happens in two steps. First the condition makes a column of True and False, then df[...] keeps only the True rows. Pick the filter that keeps the 🎯 snacks."],["Sort and take the top","sort_values puts the rows in order of one column, and .head(3) takes the first 3. Choose the column and the direction to put the 🎯 snacks on the podium, in order."],["Group and total","Which team won? groupby works in three moves: split the rows into one pile per team, apply a sum to each pile, then combine the answers. Do it for money, then for sold."],["& not and","The red team wants the snacks that cost $1 and sold more than 20. Using and crashes! Pick the fix that works."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about the bake-sale table."]],"r":["pd.read_csv(\"file.csv\") loads a table into a DataFrame: columns with names, rows with an index.","df[\"sold\"] picks a column. Maths on columns works row by row: df[\"price\"] * df[\"sold\"].","A filter is two steps: df[\"sold\"] > 15 makes a True/False column, then df[...] keeps the True rows.","df.sort_values(\"money\", ascending=False).head(3) gives the top 3.","df.groupby(\"team\")[\"money\"].sum() splits the rows into piles, adds up each pile and combines the answers.","Combine conditions with & and |, with brackets around each one: (a > 1) & (b < 5)."],"rel":["spread"]},"charts":{"k":["chart","plot","graph","matplotlib","plt","visualisation","data visualization","line chart","bar chart","scatter plot","legend"],"s":[["Numbers vs a picture","Sunny the sunflower 🌻 was measured every day. On which day did it grow the most? Try reading the numbers, then press Draw it."],["Build the chart","Each line of code adds one piece to the chart. Tap the lines to switch them on, and watch what appears. Something's odd about the last one..."],["x and y make pairs","plt.scatter(x, y) draws one dot per pair: x[0] goes with y[0], x[1] with y[1], and so on. Tap the 4 places where the dots go."],["Pick the right chart","Different questions need different charts. For each set of data, pick the chart that tells its story best."],["Charts can fib","Stand A sold 96 cups of lemonade and stand B sold 100. Stand B wants a chart that makes them look like a huge winner. Slide where the y-axis starts and see what happens."],["Two lines and a legend","Sunny 🌻 has a neighbour, Rosie. Both are on one chart now, but which line is which? Add the legend, then tap the day Sunny overtook Rosie."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about charts."]],"r":["import matplotlib.pyplot as plt, then plt.plot(x, y) draws a line through the points.","Add plt.title, plt.xlabel and plt.ylabel, and nothing appears until plt.show().","x[i] and y[i] make one point together, so both lists need the same length.","Line for change over time, bar to compare groups, scatter to see if two things are linked.","A y-axis that doesn't start at 0 can make a tiny difference look huge. Check it with plt.ylim.","Give each line a label= and call plt.legend() so readers know which is which."],"rel":["plotting"]},"random":{"k":["random","randomness","simulation","monte carlo","dice","randint","seed","shuffle","pi","chance experiments"],"s":[["Roll the dice","random.randint(1, 6) rolls a die. Roll it a few times, then let Python roll it thousands of times in a blink. What happens to the bars?"],["Both ends count","Python has two ways to pick a random whole number, and they treat the last number differently. Roll each machine 100 times and check which numbers ever come up."],["Same seed, same numbers","Computers can't really be random: they follow a recipe that starts from a number called the seed. Run the program twice, with and without random.seed(42)."],["The luckiest total","Roll two dice and add them up. Totals go from 2 to 12. Predict which total comes up most, then roll 1000 times to find out."],["Darts that find π","Throw random darts at a square board with a quarter circle on it. The share that lands inside the circle, times 4, gets close to π (3.14159…). Throw lots!"],["Shuffle the deck","Before the card game, the deck needs shuffling. This program prints None! Pick the code that shuffles the deck and prints it."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about randomness."]],"r":["import random, then random.randint(1, 6) rolls a die: both 1 and 6 can come up.","random.randrange(1, 6) works like range: it stops before 6.","A few rolls are lumpy; thousands of rolls settle into the true pattern. That's simulation.","random.seed(42) makes the same \"random\" numbers every run, which is handy for testing.","random.shuffle(cards) mixes the list in place and returns None; random.choice(cards) picks one item.","Throwing random darts can even estimate π: 4 * inside / total."],"rel":["chance","spread"]},"learner":{"k":["machine learning","ml","ai","nearest neighbour","k nearest neighbours","knn","classification","classifier","training data","accuracy","overfitting","learn from examples","model"],"s":[["Sort by looking","The market robot measured 16 fruits: size (across) and redness (up). Three new fruits arrived. Tap each ❓ and say what you think it is."],["How far is it?","A program can't \"look\", so it measures instead. math.dist gives the straight-line distance between two points. Tap examples to measure, and find the closest one to the ❓."],["The nearest-neighbour program","Here's the whole learning program, with just 4 examples to keep it short. It checks every example and remembers the closest so far. Step through it."],["Test it on new fruit","Is the program any good? Test it on 8 fruits it has never seen, whose answers we know. Slide to give it more examples and watch the accuracy."],["A wrong label","Oops: someone labelled one red apple as an orange 🍊 (dashed circle). Now one test apple gets the wrong answer. Fix it by letting the 3 nearest examples vote instead of trusting just one."],["Remembering isn't learning","With k = 1, test the program on the 17 fruits it learned from, then on the 8 new ones. Compare the scores."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions about your learning program."]],"r":["Instead of writing rules, a learning program is given labelled examples and works out new answers from them.","Each fruit is a point: (size, redness). math.dist(a, b) measures how far apart two points are.","Nearest neighbour: a new fruit gets the label of the closest example.","Test on fruit the program has never seen, and work out accuracy = correct / total.","More examples usually means better guesses. A wrong label can mislead it; letting the 3 nearest vote (k = 3) helps.","Being perfect on the training examples proves nothing: the program might just be remembering them."],"rel":["distance","descent"]},"capstone":{"k":["project","data project","data science","prediction","predict","analysis","end to end"],"s":[["Plan the project","The ice-cream van 🍦 kept a record of 12 days. Tomorrow it's going out again: how many ice creams should it bring? First, plan the program. Tap the jobs in order."],["Load and clean","Here's icecream.csv, loaded with pandas. Two rows are broken and would spoil the answers. Tap the 2 bad rows."],["Explore the data","Time to ask the clean data two questions. For each one, pick the line of code that answers it."],["Chart it","What decides how many ice creams sell? Choose what goes across the chart and look for a pattern in sold."],["Predict tomorrow","The program finds the 3 days with the closest temperature and averages what they sold, just like the fruit sorter. Tomorrow's forecast is 28°C. Then try a heatwave of 40°C."],["Run the whole program","Here's the finished program: files, pandas, a function, zip, sorted with a lambda and a slice, all working together. Step through it one last time."],["Final checkpoint","Two last questions, and then you've finished Python!"]],"r":["A data project goes: load → clean → explore → chart → predict.","df.dropna() removes rows with missing values; a filter like df[df[\"sold\"] >= 0] removes impossible ones.","df[\"sold\"].mean() and df.groupby(\"sunny\")[\"sold\"].mean() answer questions about the data.","A scatter chart showed the pattern: hotter days, more ice creams.","sorted(days, key=lambda d: abs(d[0] - temp))[:3] finds the 3 most similar days, and their average is the prediction.","A program that learns from examples can't predict far beyond what it has seen."],"rel":["lines"]},"vectors":{"k":["vector","arrow","coordinates","direction","components","x and y","linear algebra","2d"],"s":[["Jump to the rescue","Two little aliens are lost in space! Your ship's jump drive only understands two numbers: how far across and how far up. Aim with the sliders, then press Jump!"],["Two numbers, one arrow","Here's the vector [4, 3] again. Each part of it has a job. Tap the 4, the 3 and the arrow to see what each one does."],["Minus means go back","Space has no \"up\" or \"down\" walls: you can go any way. A minus sign means the opposite direction. Tap the spot where each jump lands."],["Order matters","The ship is almost out of fuel! The fuel station is 4 across and 1 up. But the pilot types the numbers the wrong way round: [1, 4]."],["Same arrow, any star","Your favourite jump is the gold one: [2, 1]. Some of the other arrows are the very same vector, just drawn from a different star. Tap all of them."],["The asteroid field","One last alien is stuck on the far side of an asteroid field. Jumps fly in straight lines, so plan a route of a few jumps that dodges every rock."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions to lock in what a vector is."]],"r":["A vector is an arrow made of numbers. [4, 3] means 4 across, then 3 up.","A minus sign means the other way: [−4, 1] goes 4 left and 1 up. A 0 means don't move that way.","Order matters: the first number is always across and the second is always up, so [1, 4] and [4, 1] are different.","A vector says how far and which way, never where to start: the same arrow can be drawn from any point.","A long trip is just a list of vectors, one after another."],"rel":["transform"]},"vecadd":{"k":["vector addition","add vectors","scaling","scalar","scalar multiplication","subtract vectors","linear algebra"],"s":[["Blown off course","Meet Ember, a young dragon who delivers letters between floating islands. She aims straight at the castle, [6, 3] away. But today it's windy… Press Fly!"],["Add the matching numbers","Now Ember plans ahead. The wind is known, so she can aim off to one side. Set her flight so that flight plus wind lands on the castle, then press Fly!"],["Either order","Yesterday Ember flew [4, 5] and then the wind pushed her [2, −2], which took her to the castle. Today the wind comes first, then she flies [4, 5]. Tap where she ends up."],["Riding the gusts","Ember is tired, so she lets the wind carry her. Each card is a gust she can ride. Pick gusts that add up to exactly the castle at [3, 4]."],["Stretch, shrink, flip","One flap of Ember's wings moves her [2, 1]. Flapping harder multiplies the whole arrow by a number. Slide the multiplier and try all three kinds of number."],["From here to there","Positions are arrows from the centre of the map: Ember's volcano home is at [−3, −1] and the castle at [4, 2]. She's at the volcano. Which jump takes her to the castle?"],["Checkpoint","Two quick sums for Ember's logbook."]],"r":["To add two vectors, put them tip to tail: the second starts where the first ends. The total goes from the very start to the very end.","In numbers, add the matching parts: [4, 5] + [2, −2] = [6, 3].","The order doesn't matter: a + b lands in the same place as b + a.","Multiplying by a number stretches (more than 1), shrinks (between 0 and 1) or flips (negative) the arrow: 2 × [2, 1] = [4, 2].","The jump from A to B is B − A: subtract the matching parts."],"rel":["dunder"]},"distance":{"k":["distance","length","magnitude","norm","pythagoras","pythagorean theorem","euclidean distance","circle"],"s":[["Which gem is closer?","Meet Bubbles, a little submarine hunting for gems. Gem A is 6 squares straight ahead. Gem B is up a diagonal. Tap the gem you think is closer, then let the sonar check."],["Squares on a staircase","Here's a 2,500-year-old trick called Pythagoras. Build a square on each step of the staircase: the two small squares together have exactly as many tiles as the big square on the arrow. Make an arrow that is exactly 5 long."],["Minus disappears","Bubbles turns around: now the arrow is [−3, 4], going left 3 and up 4. Does the minus sign change its length?"],["Between two whales","Two whales are singing, and Bubbles wants to record both songs. Her microphone only works from exactly 5 squares away. Tap a spot to move her there and check both distances."],["Crab walk or swim?","Clawdia the crab can only walk along the grid lines: across, then up. Bubbles can swim straight. Both move one square per second towards the same gem."],["The sonar circle","Bubbles' sonar beeps for anything exactly 5 squares away. There are 12 grid points like that. Tap them to plant sonar buoys. Can you find 8?"],["Checkpoint","Two quick measurements for Bubbles' logbook."]],"r":["The length of a vector is how far its arrow really goes, in a straight line.","Pythagoras: across² + up² = length². For [3, 4]: 9 + 16 = 25, so the length is √25 = 5.","A minus sign disappears when you square it ((−3)² = 9), so [−3, 4] is 5 long too.","The distance between two points is the length of the jump between them, B − A. In Python: math.dist(a, b).","Going only along the grid (across, then up) is longer: a straight line is the shortest way.","All the points at the same distance from you make a circle."],"rel":["learner"]},"bigvectors":{"k":["high dimensional","dimensions","feature vector","features","embedding","many numbers","image as numbers","linear algebra"],"s":[["A monster made of numbers","In the game Monster Cards, every monster is just a list of numbers: [speed, power, magic]. The game draws the monster from them. Build Zappix to match the card on the left."],["Three numbers make a room","Two numbers place a point on a flat map. A third number adds a third direction, like the depth of a room. Two monsters are hiding in one spot! Turn the room and tap the one that was hiding."],["Six numbers? No problem","Real monster cards have 6 stats, far too many directions to draw. But the maths doesn't care. Fuse two monsters into a team, then answer a question about a level-up potion."],["Which monster is most alike?","You own Pip [6, 3, 8, 2] (speed, power, magic, armour). Which rival is the most similar to Pip? Pick one, then let the game measure the distance."],["A picture is a vector","This is a tiny 4 × 4 picture of a ghost monster. Each square is a number: 0 is black, 9 is white. Press Unroll to turn the picture into a vector."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions to finish the chapter."]],"r":["A vector can have any number of numbers. A monster [speed, power, magic] is a vector with 3.","With 3 numbers, each vector is a point in a room (3 directions). One more number = one more direction.","We can't draw 6 or 784 directions, but the maths still works: add and multiply each matching number.","Distance works too: square every difference, add them all up, then take the square root.","Lots of small differences can add up to less than one big difference.","A picture is a vector too: unroll its pixel numbers into one long list. A 28 × 28 photo is a vector of 784 numbers."],"rel":["numpy","cosine"]},"dotproduct":{"k":["dot product","scalar product","projection","multiply and add","score","linear algebra"],"s":[["Wind in the sails","Ahoy! Captain Coral sails due east (brown arrow). A magic wind can blow from any direction. Turn the wind and watch how much it pushes the ship along its course."],["The wind's shadow","Imagine the sun straight overhead: the wind arrow casts a shadow onto the course line. That shadow is exactly the push. Tap a point to set the wind, and find two different winds that push exactly 3 forward."],["Multiply and add","Now the ship sails a slanted course, [2, 1]. To measure the push, use the dot product: multiply the matching numbers, then add. Make the dot product exactly 0 with a real wind."],["Read the sign","The sign of the dot product tells you everything. For each logbook entry, work out the dot product of the course and the wind, then decide: does the wind help, go sideways, or fight the ship?"],["Two surprises","The dot product has two tricks up its sleeve. Try to predict both."],["Which island?","Each island is a vector [gold, danger, distance]. Each captain has their own wishes: a number for how much they care about each one. Island score = island · wishes. Pick the best island for each captain."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions for the ship's log."]],"r":["Only the part of the wind that points along your course pushes you forward. It's the wind's shadow on the course line.","The dot product of two vectors: multiply the matching numbers, then add: [3, 1] · [2, 2] = 3×2 + 1×2 = 8.","Positive = roughly the same way. Zero = at right angles (sideways). Negative = roughly opposite.","Longer arrows make bigger dot products, and a vector dotted with itself is its length squared: [3, 4] · [3, 4] = 25.","Scoring things with features · weights is exactly what an artificial neuron does."],"rel":["weighted","matrix"]},"cosine":{"k":["cosine similarity","cosine","angle","similarity","recommendation","recommender","embeddings","word vectors","word2vec"],"s":[["Music twins?","At Zorb's Café, each alien rates two kinds of music, so each alien is a point [robot-pop, space-jazz]. Zib (pink) wants a music twin. Who has the most similar taste?"],["Direction, not strength","Glorp loves both kinds of music equally: [2, 2]. Set your ratings and watch the angle between your arrows. Find two different taste twins for Glorp."],["The similarity meter","The café turns the angle into one number with a dot product: a · b ÷ (length of a × length of b). Dividing by the lengths cancels out how strongly you feel. Make the meter read +1, 0 and −1."],["Four kinds of music","The café adds two more kinds of music, so tastes now have 4 numbers: too many to draw, but the maths is the same. Quix has just arrived. Which alien is Quix's best music twin?"],["Words are arrows too","AI turns words into vectors the same way. On this tiny word map, across means \"cat-like\" and up means \"baby animal\". Do some arrow maths: start at puppy, take away dog, then add cat."],["Checkpoint","Two last questions to finish Chapter 2."]],"r":["Two tastes are alike when their arrows point the same way, even if one is much longer (feels more strongly).","So compare the angle between arrows, not the distance between dots.","Cosine similarity = a · b ÷ (length of a × length of b). It's always between −1 and 1.","1 = same direction (twins), 0 = right angles (unrelated), −1 = opposite directions.","It works with any number of ratings, and it's how AI compares word vectors: puppy − dog + cat ≈ kitten."],"rel":["bigvectors","nextword"]},"potions":{"k":["function","f(x)","input output","mapping","composition","rule"],"s":[["The mystery machine","Welcome to Wizard Willow's potion workshop! This machine takes a number of frog eyes 🐸 and pours out a potion. Its rule is a secret. Feed it 3 different numbers and watch what comes out."],["Writing it down","Willow writes her machines as tiny formulas. This is the tripler: f(x) = 3 × x. Tap each part to see what it means."],["Build a machine","Willow needs a new machine that matches her recipe book (the wanted column). Turn the two dials until every row matches."],["Machines in a row","Willow chains two machines: +1 then ×2. The output of the first drops straight into the second. Predict what comes out when you feed in 3."],["Crack the secret rule","A rival wizard left a machine with a secret rule. Feed it any numbers you like, then test your guesses against all your results. Finding a rule from examples is exactly what machine learning does!"],["The mood machine","Willow's old mood machine is grumpy. Feed it 2 twice and compare the potions."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions for Willow's spellbook."]],"r":["A function is a machine with a rule: every input gives exactly one output, the same one every time.","We write it as f(x) = 3 × x: f is the machine's name, x is the input, and the rule says what to do with it.","f(5) means \"feed 5 into f\": f(5) = 15.","Chaining machines feeds one's output into the next, and the order matters: (+1 then ×2) isn't (×2 then +1).","Working out a machine's rule from examples of inputs and outputs is exactly what machine learning does."],"rel":["variables","functions","params"]},"plotting":{"k":["graph","plot","coordinates","x axis","y axis","chart","data points"],"s":[["Launch day","The rocket club is launching Zoomy! Its flight computer writes down the height every second. Press Launch."],["Dots on a grid","Turn the log into a picture! Each row becomes one dot: go across to the time, then up to the height. The first dot is done. Tap where the next 4 go."],["Join the dots","Join the dots and you get Zoomy's graph: the whole flight in one picture. Slide through time and find the peak, and both moments when Zoomy was 60 metres up."],["Every graph tells a story","The club logged four other flying things. Each story makes a different shape. Match each story to its graph."],["Between the dots","The flight computer only wrote down whole seconds. A judge asks: \"How high was Zoomy at 2.5 seconds?\" Use the graph."],["A machine makes a picture","Remember Willow's machines? Feed f(x) = 2 × x + 1 the inputs 0 to 5 and plot each result as a dot: input across, output up."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions for the rocket club."]],"r":["A graph turns every (input, output) pair of a function into a dot: input across, output up.","(3, 75) means \"at 3 seconds, the height was 75 metres\".","Joining the dots shows the whole story: going up, the peak, coming down.","Two different inputs can share an output (60 m on the way up and on the way down).","A smooth curve lets you read values between the dots you measured.","Some functions make dots in a perfectly straight line: those are next!"],"rel":["charts"]},"lines":{"k":["straight line","linear","linear function","y = mx + b","slope","intercept","gradient","line of best fit","linear regression","regression","fitting"],"s":[["Build a ramp","Skye wants a ramp from the starting box (1 high) up to the top deck (5 high, 4 steps across). Set the ramp with two dials, then press Ride!"],["b: up and down","This ramp has steepness 1. Slide b and watch the whole ramp move up and down without tilting. Collect all 3 coins 🪙, one at a time."],["m: the steepness","Now keep the start fixed at 2 and turn m. The orange steps show what m means: for every 1 step across, the ramp goes m up. Make an uphill, a flat and a downhill ramp."],["Through two posts","Skye wants a grind rail that touches the tops of two posts: (1, 3) and (3, 7). Look at the gap between the posts to work out m, then find b."],["The line of best fit","Skye logged hours of practice and tricks landed. Real data is messy, so no line hits every dot. Slide the dials to make the total miss (the red gaps) as small as you can."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions to finish building the park."]],"r":["Every straight line is y = m × x + b: two numbers describe the whole line.","b is the starting height: where the line crosses the up-axis (when x is 0).","m is the steepness: how far the line goes up for each 1 step across. 0 is flat; negative goes downhill.","Two points are enough to pin down a line: m = up ÷ across between them.","The line of best fit is the m and b that miss the data dots the least. Finding it is the first machine-learning model."],"rel":["capstone","slope","descent"]},"curves":{"k":["curve","exponential","doubling","quadratic","parabola","sigmoid","logistic","relu","activation function","nonlinear"],"s":[["Two jars of slime","In Dr Gloop's lab, jar A starts with 10 slime and gains 10 more every day. Jar B starts with just 1, but it doubles every day. Slide through the days."],["Doubling on a graph","Here are both jars as graphs. Jar A makes a straight line; jar B hugs the floor, then shoots up. Slide the days and see it happen."],["The slime bowl","Dr Gloop's slime slide is shaped by y = x × x. Roll the ball along: x is how far across, y is the height. Find the bottom, and both places where the ball is 9 high."],["The squeeze tube","This tube squeezes any number, however huge, into one between 0 and 1. Feed it different numbers and watch the output. Make it say almost 1, almost 0, and exactly 0.5."],["The one-way valve","The lab's slime valve only opens one way: negative numbers come out as 0, and positive numbers go straight through unchanged. Try it, then answer."],["Checkpoint","Two last questions to finish Chapter 3."]],"r":["Adding the same amount each time makes a straight line. Multiplying (like doubling) makes a curve that explodes upwards: exponential growth.","Doubling 20 times turns 1 into more than a million.","The bowl y = x × x has a lowest point at the bottom. Finding the bottom of a bowl is how AI learns (next chapter!).","The squeeze tube (sigmoid) turns any number into one between 0 and 1, perfect for \"how sure am I?\".","The one-way valve (ReLU) turns negative numbers into 0 and lets positive numbers through."],"rel":["bigo","search","weighted","zoom"]},"slope":{"k":["slope","gradient","steepness","rise over run","incline","uphill","downhill"],"s":[["Which trail is steeper?","Billy the mountain goat has two trails. Trail A climbs higher; trail B is shorter. Send Billy up both, then decide which one is steeper."],["Rise over run","Slope = up ÷ across. People also say rise over run. Tap the grid to plant Billy's flag and see that trail's slope. Find 3 different summits with a slope of exactly 2."],["Read it like a book","A slope can be positive (uphill), 0 (flat) or negative (downhill). The one rule: always read a trail from left to right, like a line of text. What kind of slope does each trail have?"],["From flat to cliff","Tilt the trail and watch its slope. Make the slope exactly 1, then push it above 10, then go all the way to a cliff."],["A mountain of many slopes","Real mountains change as you go. Billy's mountain has 5 sections. Tap each section to measure its slope."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions before Billy heads home."]],"r":["Steepness is not how high or how long a trail is: it's how much it goes up for each step across. That number is the slope.","Slope = rise ÷ run (up ÷ across). Short and long trails can share the same slope.","Read slopes left to right: uphill is positive, flat is 0, downhill is negative.","Bigger numbers are steeper. A straight-up cliff has 0 across, so it has no slope number (you can't divide by 0).","Real mountains (and real graphs) change slope from one section to the next."],"rel":["lines"]},"rate":{"k":["rate of change","speed","velocity","distance time graph","average speed","per"],"s":[["Drive and draw","Meet Zip, a toy race car. The finish line is 30 m away and you have 8 seconds. Start the clock, then switch gears as Zip drives. Watch the graph draw itself!"],["The speedometer in the slope","Here is Zip's graph from a practice lap. Slide through time. For each straight part, the slope is metres ÷ seconds, and the speedometer shows it. When was Zip fastest?"],["Who's who?","Three new racers ran for 8 seconds: a snail, a dog and a cheetah. Each line is one of them. Use the slopes to match each line to its racer."],["Not a picture of the road!","Here is Zip's graph from another trip. It looks just like a hill. Before you play the trip, predict what Zip actually did."],["The steady twin","Zip sped up and slowed down on this lap. Eddie drives at one steady speed the whole way. Set Eddie's speed so the race ends in a perfect tie after 8 seconds."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions to finish the race."]],"r":["A distance–time graph shows how far something is from the start at each moment.","Its slope is metres ÷ seconds: the speed. Steeper means faster, flat means standing still.","Slope is a rate of change: how fast one thing changes as another (here, time) goes by.","A graph is not a picture of the road. Going down on a distance graph means coming back: a negative slope.","Average speed = total distance ÷ total time: the slope of the straight line from start to finish."],"rel":[]},"zoom":{"k":["derivative","calculus","tangent","tangent line","instantaneous","differentiation","maximum","minimum","turning point"],"s":[["Ride the coaster","Welcome to Loopy Land! Slide the cart along the track and watch it tilt. Find a spot where it's climbing hard, the flat top, and a steep dive."],["Zoom in!","Point your magnifying glass at one spot on the track and zoom in. Keep going until the track looks perfectly straight."],["Close the gap","Here's another way. Draw a straight line through point P and a second point Q on the track, and measure its slope (up ÷ across). Now slide Q closer and closer to P. What does the slope settle on?"],["Find the flat spots","Now a touching ruler rides with the cart and shows the slope at every point. Find the two places where the slope is exactly 0: the very top and the very bottom."],["Slopes make a graph","Let's record the slope at every point of the ride. Slide the cart from start to end: the bottom graph plots the slope at each spot. Then answer the question."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions before you leave Loopy Land."]],"r":["A straight line has one slope. A curve has a different slope at every point.","Zoom in far enough on a smooth curve and it looks like a straight line. Its slope is the slope at that point.","Slide a second point closer and closer: the line through both becomes the tangent line (the touching line). Its slope is the derivative.","At the very top and the very bottom of a curve, the slope is 0.","The slopes at every point make a new graph: above 0 when climbing, below 0 when falling, crossing 0 at tops and bottoms."],"rel":["curves"]},"descent":{"k":["gradient descent","optimisation","optimization","minimise","loss","cost function","error","learning rate","training","how ai learns","machine learning","step size"],"s":[["Lost in the murk","Inky the octopus dropped a pearl, and it rolled to the deepest point of Murky Lake. The water is too dark to see far; Inky can only feel the ground. Step left and right to find the pearl."],["Which way is down?","Now it's pitch black. All Inky has is a number: the slope under its tentacles (read left to right, like always). Choose which way Inky should go to get deeper."],["Let the slope choose the step","The water has cleared a little. Here's a clever recipe: new spot = spot − slope. Steep ground means a big step; gentle ground means a small one. Press Take a step until Inky reaches the bottom."],["Goldilocks steps","Now we add a dial: new spot = spot − step size × slope. Try all four step sizes and watch Inky dive 8 steps with each. Which one is just right?"],["Two dips","This lake has two dips. You can see them, but Inky can't! Tap the water to drop Inky anywhere, and gradient descent takes over. Find a spot where Inky gets stuck, and one where it finds the pearl."],["This is how AI learns","Remember Skye's line of best fit? The total miss for each steepness m makes a bowl. Inky is riding it! Press Learn and the computer turns the m dial by itself, using gradient descent."],["Checkpoint","Two last questions to finish Chapter 4."]],"r":["At the lowest point of a curve, the slope is 0.","To go downhill, step the opposite way to the slope: positive slope → go left, negative slope → go right.","Gradient descent: new spot = spot − step size × slope. Steep ground gives big steps, flat ground tiny ones.","The step size is the learning rate: too small crawls, too big zigzags or flies off.","Gradient descent can get stuck in a small dip: a local minimum.","AI learns by doing gradient descent on its total miss, turning millions of dials downhill at once."],"rel":["learner","lines"]},"weighted":{"k":["weighted sum","weights","bias","neuron","perceptron","artificial neuron","neural network","score","weighted average"],"s":[["Judge Hoot's scorecard","Welcome to the Forest Talent Show! Each act gets three ratings out of 10. Judge Hoot doesn't just add them up: he multiplies each one by how much he cares (its weight). Score all 3 acts."],["Turn the knobs","Now you are the judge. Turn the three weight knobs and watch the scores change. Can you make each act win, one at a time?"],["The ignored superstar","A new act! Polly the parrot got two perfect 10s. Today Hoot's weights are skill ×3, fun ×0, noise ×−1. Predict who Hoot scores higher."],["A thumb on the scale","Hoot's mood adds the same number to every score: that's called a bias. An act needs 15 or more for a golden ticket 🎫. Find a mood where only Pip gets one, then one where everyone does."],["The buzzer is a neuron","Here's Hoot's whole decision as a picture: three ratings flow in along wires (weights), get added up with the bias, and the buzzer goes off if the total is 0 or more. Invent your own act with the sliders."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions before the curtain falls."]],"r":["A weighted sum: multiply each number by its weight, then add everything up.","A weight says how much you care: big = a lot, 0 = ignore it, negative = dislike it.","Change the weights and you change the winner. A huge rating times a weight of 0 is still 0.","A bias is a number added to every score: a thumb on the scale that makes \"yes\" easier or harder.","Weighted sum + bias + a yes/no buzzer = an artificial neuron, the building block of neural networks."],"rel":["dotproduct","curves"]},"matrix":{"k":["matrix","matrices","matrix multiplication","matrix vector","layer","neural network layer","dense layer","linear algebra"],"s":[["One robot, three jobs","Welcome to Professor Cog's robot factory! Each robot has three stats. Each job cares about them differently (its weights). Score Zippy for all three jobs."],["Stack them into a matrix","Write each job's weights as a row, and stack the rows into one table: a matrix. Zippy's stats stand in a column. Press Next row and watch each row meet the column: multiply the pairs, then add."],["Shapes must match","Professor Cog keeps adding jobs and stats. Change the shapes with the + and − buttons. Find a shape that breaks the machine, then make one that scores 4 jobs at once."],["The whole factory at once","Line up all three robots as columns. Now the matrix meets every column: 9 scores in one go. Run the factory, then tap the best robot for each job."],["A layer of neurons","Give every job row a bias of −11 and a buzzer: a robot is hired if its score reaches 11. Each row is now a neuron, and the whole matrix is a layer. Build a robot that gets only the Rescue job, then one that gets every job."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions before the factory closes for the night."]],"r":["Scoring one robot for several jobs = several weighted sums of the same numbers.","Stack the jobs' weights into a table: that's a matrix. Each row is one job.","Matrix × vector: each row meets the column of numbers, multiply pairs and add. You get one answer per row.","Shapes must match: each row needs exactly as many weights as the vector has numbers.","Matrix × many robots fills a whole score table at once. Add a bias and buzzer to every row and the matrix is a layer of neurons."],"rel":["numpy","dotproduct"]},"transform":{"k":["linear transformation","transformation","rotation","reflection","shear","determinant","matrix","linear algebra"],"s":[["The mirror maze","Welcome to the carnival's funhouse! Whiskers the cat stands in front of each mirror. Try at least 4 mirrors and watch the numbers under the picture."],["Follow the ear","Here's the recipe for every point: new x = a×x + b×y and new y = c×x + d×y. Whiskers' ear tip starts at (1, 1.5). Set the four numbers to land the ear on the ⭐, then on the 🍩."],["Where do the arrows land?","Here's the secret: the first column is where the red → arrow lands, and the second column is where the blue ↑ arrow lands. Pick an arrow, then tap the grid to move its tip. Make Whiskers match the dashed outline."],["Spin the cat","A spinning mirror! Turn it and watch the four numbers. Spin Whiskers a half turn so she's upside down, then answer the question."],["Squashed flat!","The last mirror is broken: the red → arrow is stuck at (2, 1). Tap the grid to move the blue ↑ arrow, and watch Whiskers' size meter. Can you squash her completely flat?"],["Checkpoint","Two last questions to finish Chapter 5."]],"r":["A 2×2 matrix moves every point: new x = a·x + b·y, new y = c·x + d·y. Each new coordinate is a weighted sum of the old ones.","The first column is where the → arrow lands; the second column is where the ↑ arrow lands. Those two arrows decide everything.","Stretching, flipping, spinning and leaning are all just four numbers.","A mirror flip swaps left and right; a half-turn spin doesn't, even though both put the picture upside down.","If both arrows land on one line, the picture gets squashed flat, and no matrix can undo it: information is lost."],"rel":["vectors"]},"chance":{"k":["probability","chance","odds","likelihood","random","outcomes","gambler's fallacy","law of large numbers"],"s":[["Open the chests","Goblin Gus sells treasure chests. Each one drops a gold coin, a potion or, if you're lucky, a crown. Open at least 20 chests and keep count."],["From impossible to certain","Every chance is a number from 0 to 1. 0 means impossible, 1 means certain. Give each event its chance and watch it land on the line."],["Design a chest","Gus lets you pack your own chest! Use + and − to choose how many of each token go in. First make the crown chance exactly 0.25. Then make a chest where coins and potions are 0.5 each."],["The crown is \"due\"!","Gus's customer Bram opened 5 chests and got 🪙 🪙 🪙 🪙 🪙. \"Five coins in a row!\" he shouts. \"A crown must be coming next!\" Is he right?"],["The real chance appears","Open chests in big batches and watch the line: it shows the share of crowns so far. Keep going until you've opened 1,000 chests."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions before Gus closes his shop."]],"r":["Chance (probability) = matching outcomes ÷ all outcomes, when every outcome is equally likely.","Chances go from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain). 0.5 is an even chance.","All the chances of all the possible outcomes add up to 1.","A chest has no memory: a streak of coins doesn't make a crown \"due\".","Open enough chests and the share of crowns gets closer and closer to the real chance. AI learns chances by counting lots of examples."],"rel":["random","nextword"]},"combine":{"k":["and probability","or probability","independent events","multiply probabilities","complement","at least one","risk"],"s":[["Run the dungeon","Sir Squeak trains mouse knights in a dungeon with two traps. The 🔥 fire jets get a mouse half the time; the 🐍 snake bites 1 time in 3. (Caught mice just get sent home, a bit embarrassed.) Send in 60 mice."],["AND means multiply","Here are 6 equally likely worlds: 2 ways the fire can go × 3 ways the snake can go. Tap every world where Sir Squeak gets through both traps."],["OR: don't count twice","Now tap every world where at least one trap gets him: the fire, the snake, or both."],["Small risks pile up","A longer dungeon has lots of little traps. Each one is safe 9 times in 10 (0.9). Add traps and watch Sir Squeak's chance of getting through. With how many traps does it first drop below half?"],["Pick the safer route","Two routes to the cheese vault 🧀. Route A has one big trap, safe half the time (0.5). Route B has three small traps, each safe 0.8. Which route should Sir Squeak take?"],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions before Sir Squeak hangs up his sword."]],"r":["List every equally likely world; a chance is just the share of worlds where your event happens.","AND (both things happen, when they don't affect each other): multiply the chances. ½ × ⅔ = ⅓.","OR (at least one happens): you can't just add, because the \"both\" worlds get counted twice. Easiest: 1 − chance of neither.","The chance of something not happening is 1 − its chance.","Many small risks multiply into a big one: 0.9 safe, ten times in a row, is only about 0.35."],"rel":["logic"]},"spread":{"k":["average","mean","spread","variance","standard deviation","histogram","bell curve","normal distribution","distribution","statistics"],"s":[["Two archers","Welcome to the Woodland Archery Tournament! 🦊 Fox and 🐻 Bear shoot 8 arrows each. Let them both shoot, then decide which archer's arrows are centred on the bullseye on average."],["The balance point","Fox's scores in five rounds were 7, 9, 8, 10 and 6. Each score is a ball on a seesaw. Slide the pivot until the seesaw balances: that spot is the average."],["Same average, different spread","In the final, both archers averaged 8 points. Is that a tie? For each archer, shrink the green band around 8 until it's as narrow as possible while still holding every score."],["The bell appears","🦔 Hattie the hedgehog aims at the middle every time, but lots of tiny wobbles (wind, a sneeze, a twitchy paw) push each arrow a bit left or right. Shoot 300 arrows and watch where they pile up."],["Shape the bell","Every bell curve is set by just two numbers: the average (where it's centred) and the spread (how wide it is). Turn both dials to match the dashed bell of the tournament champion."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions before the medals are handed out."]],"r":["The average (mean) = add everything up, divide by how many. It's the balance point of the data.","The spread says how far the values usually are from the average. Same average can mean very different spreads.","You need both: an archer can be steady but aim off (small spread, wrong average) or aim true but wobble.","Lots of small random wobbles added together make a bell curve: most values near the middle, few far away.","Two numbers, the average and the spread, describe a whole bell curve."],"rel":["random","pandas"]},"bayes":{"k":["bayes","bayes theorem","bayesian","conditional probability","base rate","evidence","update","spam filter","naive bayes"],"s":[["A creak in the night","Lady Mabel lives in a spooky castle. On most nights it's just the wind, but sometimes a ghost drifts by. Ghosts make the old door creak 9 times out of 10. Tonight, she hears a creak! What's your gut feeling?"],["Count the creaky nights","Here are 100 nights: 10 ghost nights 👻 and 90 windy nights 💨. Press the button to keep only the nights with a creak, then count who's left."],["A second clue","Lady Mabel notices another clue: a sudden chill 🥶. Ghosts bring a chill 8 times in 10; the wind only 1 time in 10. Switch the clues on and off and watch the ghost meter. Try the creak alone, then both clues."],["Rare things stay rare","What if ghosts visited more (or less) often? Slide how many nights in 100 have a ghost, and watch what a single creak means. Find the fewest ghost nights where a creak means \"probably a ghost\" (50% or more)."],["The castle mail sorter","Lady Mabel gets lots of junk letters. Her robot sorter starts out thinking 1 letter in 10 is junk, and each word is a clue. Tap words to add them to the letter. Make the sorter 90% sure it's junk, then write a letter it's 95% sure is real."],["Checkpoint","Two quick questions before the candles go out."]],"r":["To judge a clue, count: among all the times you'd see this clue, how often is the cause the one you care about?","A clue that's likely for ghosts can still point to the wind, if windy nights are much more common.","Each new clue updates the chance: two clues together can be far more convincing than one.","How common something is to start with (the base rate) matters a lot: rare things stay fairly unlikely.","This is Bayes' rule, and AI uses it everywhere, from spam filters to medical tests."],"rel":[]},"nextword":{"k":["language model","llm","chatgpt","chatbot","next word prediction","text generation","temperature","softmax","bigram","generative ai","tokens"],"s":[["Spin for the next word","Meet Tale-o-Tron, a story machine. It has started a story: \"Once upon a…\". It doesn't know what comes next for sure, so it spins a wheel: bigger slices are more likely. Spin 5 times."],["Learning the chances","To learn what comes after \"the cat\", Tale-o-Tron reads lots of sentences and counts. Help it read its little library, one sentence at a time."],["Write a story","Now write with Tale-o-Tron! At each step you see the chances for the next word. Tap a word to choose it yourself, or press 🎲 Roll to let the dice decide. Finish 2 stories."],["The temperature dial","Tale-o-Tron has a temperature dial. Low makes likely words even likelier; high evens the chances out. Write 10 endings for \"The dragon…\" with the dial cold (0.5 or less), then hot (1.5 or more)."],["Scores into chances","Inside Tale-o-Tron, a neural network gives every possible next word a score (built from weighted sums, like Judge Hoot's). Then it turns scores into chances: bigger scores get much bigger shares, and the shares always add to 1. Make \"dragon\" win more than half, then make all three equally likely."],["Checkpoint","Two last questions to finish Math for AI!"]],"r":["A chatbot writes one word at a time, choosing the next word like spinning a weighted wheel.","The chances come from counting what follows each phrase in enormous amounts of text.","Rolling the dice (instead of always picking the top word) keeps stories fresh.","The temperature dial: low = safe and predictable, high = wild and surprising.","Inside, the machine gives every word a score (weighted sums!), then squeezes the scores into chances that add up to 1."],"rel":["dicts","cosine","chance"]}}};
